window.ARCHIVE_EN = {"articles": {"001": {"title": "The True Meaning of Practice", "summary": "From counting recitations and prostrations back to the arising of each thought and the good done in daily life.", "blocks": ["One night I dreamed of Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva. In the dream I asked: if practice is not about how much good we do, how many times we bow to the Buddha, or even how many sutras we recite, then what exactly is it that we cultivate? Are we not born into this world in order to practice?", "\"Practice is not ten thousand recitations of the mouth,\nnor ten thousand bows of the hands;\nnor counting merit like beads on an abacus,\nnor clutching sutras like amulets.\nOne whose heart is free of greed, anger and delusion,\nwho is ever mindful of the suffering of beings — this is deep practice.\nOne who never bows before the Buddha,\nyet whose every thought turns toward good, who pities others with a compassionate heart — this too is practice.\"\n—\n\"What you cultivate is nothing outside the Dharma;\nyou cultivate your own original Buddha-nature.\nLike the lotus rising from the mud, like a mirror that was never dusty,\nall sutras, all holy images, all recitation and giving are but one gate:\n— to return you to your source, to see your nature and become Buddha.\"\n—\n\"Ask not how many sutras or how many bows; ask whether your heart is stained with dust.\nSeek not swift blessings; seek that every thought leave nothing to be ashamed of and be free of folly.\"\n(End of the dream teaching.)"]}, "002": {"title": "Wisdom for Outer Circumstances and Inner Peace", "summary": "When looks, money and the way we speak become burdens, how to see our own worth anew.", "blocks": ["One night in a dream I again received a teaching from Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva.\nI said: \"Bodhisattva, you once taught me not to belittle myself over outward circumstances. Yet most people care deeply about appearance, finances, how well they speak — and above all about their own inner adjustment. Not everyone is able to calm themselves and release pressure, and not everyone has the chance to meet the Dharma. Would you teach once more, for the world and for me?\"", "\"Beings take form to be real and appearance to be true, not knowing that form is illusory and the mind is the root. Look at yourself in a mirror and you see dust, and you grieve; look at yourself with wisdom and you see the Buddha, and you are at peace. Your doubt is the common doubt of all. Know this: outer form waxes and wanes, but the heart within neither arises nor ceases. Wealth and poverty are shadows cast by conditions; only the light within the heart is an indestructible treasure.\nTherefore I say: do not take poverty as suffering, do not take your looks as shame. If the heart is ever pure and the mind ever reflects upon itself, then though the form is ordinary, its bearing is noble; though the words are clumsy, the truth stands; though the life is crooked, the virtue is straight. If a person can turn a single thought and see that they lack nothing, a thousand obstacles fall away at once. The practitioner does not seek to change the outer form, only the form of the heart; once the flower of the heart opens, all the heavens are fragrant.\"\nThe Bodhisattva asked me to remind the world as well:\n\"A person is honored not for their looks, but valued for their heart.\"\nWhoever keeps the light of their own heart amid illusory appearances, though ordinary, is close to the sage.\nThis teaching is not only for me; it is for everyone with the karmic affinity to read it — all who strive to cultivate the heart in this world, yet are so often shaken by outward things."]}, "003": {"title": "The Truth About \"Not Being Able to Let Go\"", "summary": "Amid wealth, reputation and family love, practicing love without binding and protection without control.", "blocks": ["One day in a dream I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: could you teach the world what attachment is? How do you see people's attachment to wealth, power, desire, status — and the attachment of mothers and fathers to their children?", "\"The attachment of beings is like a fly clinging to honey: sweet at the first taste, stuck fast thereafter. To be attached is to cling to the self, and to cling to what the self possesses. If one knows that all things arise from conditions and are impermanent, there is nothing to gain and nothing to lose. Wealth is but a brief gathering, power but a passing shadow, beauty but a borrowed form, fame but a sound on the wind. A parent's love for a child is born of affection too; without understanding causes and conditions, love becomes a bond. If you can love without binding and protect without controlling, then affection becomes compassion and the bond is released. What the world calls 'not being able to let go' is not that the thing is heavy — it is that the heart is heavy. If the heart can turn, iron chains become flowers, and the sea of suffering becomes a boat.\"", "The Bodhisattva said: the attachment of worldly people is like a fly craving honey. At first it tastes sweet, but once you are stuck, it is hard to get free. To be \"attached\" really means being bound by \"me\" and \"mine\" — treating the self and everything we own as solid and unchanging.\nIf we understand that everything comes together through causes and conditions and is always changing, we will see that both gaining and losing are only illusions. Wealth is just something gathered for a while; power is a brief shadow; beauty is the outer appearance of a borrowed body; fame is no more than wind passing the ear.\nAs for parents' love for their children — it is the deepest bond in the human world. But without understanding causes, conditions and impermanence, this love turns into a restraint. If you can love without binding and protect without controlling, that deep affection turns into compassion, and the bond that once restrained it naturally comes undone.\nPeople often say \"I can't let go.\" In truth, it is not that the thing is too heavy, but that the heart is too heavy. If you can shift your mind — turning attachment into compassion and worry into blessing — the chains that once bound you become flowers, and even the sea of suffering becomes a boat that carries yourself and others across."]}, "004": {"title": "Is \"Free Will\" Truly Free?", "summary": "Through the image of a mirror and its reflections, an inquiry into consciousness, will and the freedom to choose.", "blocks": ["One night I again dreamed of the Bodhisattva. I asked: What is consciousness? What is will? Are the decisions we make really our own? Do people truly have free will?", "The Bodhisattva replied: \"In asking about consciousness and will, you probe the very root of the mind. Consciousness is like the image in a mirror, appearing according to conditions; will is like the angle of the mirror, deciding what it reflects. Yet the minds of beings are mostly stained with dust; when the mirror is unclear, all that it shows is illusion. What you call 'free will', in ordinary life, is mostly moved by the winds of karma — it seems to decide for itself, but in truth it follows conditions. Yet if a single thought is pure, knowing karma without being turned by karma, that is true freedom. Thus the difference between sage and ordinary person lies not in the measure of intelligence, but in whether the mind can be its own master within the stream of cause and effect.\"\nThe Bodhisattva's second reply: \"All dharmas are mind alone; all phenomena are consciousness alone. What is called consciousness is a shadow arising from the six sense objects; it is born as consciousness meets its object, like waves chasing the wind, with no substance of its own. Will is where the mind is directed, what consciousness takes hold of — sometimes clear, sometimes turbid, flowing on with karma; it is no permanent master. Beings say 'I am the one who acts', yet in truth it is the coming together of causes and conditions, built jointly by karma and consciousness. Your thought today is shaped where past causes and present conditions meet; it is not a thought that arises alone. But if the mind becomes clear and karma is extinguished, if consciousness is transformed into wisdom, then though you still accord with cause and effect, you are free with respect to karma. So to the question of whether people have free will, the answer is: the ordinary person has the freedom of conditioned arising, not ultimate ease; the awakened one has the freedom of release from karma, which is true liberation. The ordinary person's choices seem self-directed yet are led by karma; the sage's actions accord with karma yet are not bound by it. This is called true freedom.\"", "Plain rendering of the first reply:\n\"Consciousness\" is like a reflection in a mirror: whatever is in front of it, it shows.\nIt is a reaction stirred up by the outside world, not a force that arises on its own.\n\"Will\" is like the angle of the mirror — it decides which way to point.\nIt is the mind's leaning and power of choice, which can steer what you pay attention to and what you pursue.\nBut most people's minds are clouded by dust — greed, anger, delusion, old habits and emotions.\nWhen the mirror is not clear, the images it shows are naturally distorted, confused and unreal. What you call \"free will\" is, in daily life, mostly pushed by the wind of karma.\nIt looks as though you are making the decision, but you are really just drifting with conditions.\nPeople think \"I want to do this\", when really \"past habits are pushing you to think this way.\"\nSo for most people, \"freedom\" is an illusion —\nlike a boat in the wind that thinks it is sailing, when it is only being pushed by the waves. But if one thought becomes clear and aware, understanding how karma works,\nand you are no longer led by it — that is true freedom. Freedom is not doing whatever you like;\nit is \"when desire comes, not following it; when a thought arises, being aware of it.\"\nKnowing \"this is karma moving\" and yet not being carried away — this is the strength of the awakened.\nSo the difference between a sage and an ordinary person is not how clever or learned they are,\nbut whether, in the flow of cause and effect, the mind can stay its own master and not be swept along.\nPlain rendering of the second reply:\n\"Everything in the world really comes from the mind and consciousness.\nWhat we call 'consciousness' is like an image or reaction produced when our senses (eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, mind) meet the outside world. It is like wind raising waves on water — the waves do not make themselves; they rise as soon as the wind comes.\n'Will' is the direction of the mind — the power of consciousness that leads us to act one way or another. But this will is not always clear; it is clouded by karma, emotion and desire, sometimes pure, sometimes murky.\nPeople think 'I can control everything', but mostly it is the result of many conditions together — old habits, present surroundings, the thought of the moment.\nAny thought or decision you have today is woven from past experience, emotion, memory and the situation in front of you; it does not arise alone.\nBut if your mind can become clear, see through how karma works and no longer be led by it, then although you still live within cause and effect, you can be free of karma and no longer controlled by it.\nSo if you ask, 'Do people truly have free will?' the answer is:\nOrdinary people have a 'freedom that follows conditions' — they seem to choose, but are still moved by karma;\nonly the awakened have a 'freedom beyond karma' — they act in accord with cause and effect, yet are no longer bound by it.\nThe freedom of ordinary people is not ultimate; the freedom of the sage is true liberation.\""]}, "005": {"title": "What Is the Meaning of Life?", "summary": "Between gain and loss, fame and fortune, finding a direction for life that can be lived out day by day.", "blocks": ["I would like to ask the Bodhisattva to tell the world what the meaning of life is.", "\"The meaning of life lies not in gain or loss, but in awakening.\nThe world chases fame and profit like chasing a reflection on water — grasped, yet never real;\nthe wise cultivate the nature of the mind like planting a tree in the earth — slow, yet true.\nA human life arises from conditions; it is not by chance.\nYou come into this life not to contend for high and low, but to understand the source of suffering and joy.\nWealth is not evil, poverty is not suffering;\nevil lies in losing the original heart, suffering in not knowing when enough is enough.\nPractice is not abandoning the world and leaving ordinary life;\nit is knowing the mind amid the ordinary, growing wisdom amid suffering, keeping steady amid chaos.\nIf you are not deluded by fame and profit, not attached in love and desire,\nnot moved by honor and disgrace, not resentful in hardship —\nthis is called true practice; this is the meaning of life.\nYou ask what life is for.\nI say: 'To awaken and do good — that is all.'\"", "The Bodhisattva said:\nThe meaning of life is not about how much you win or lose, but whether you can awaken.\nPeople chase fame and profit like chasing a reflection in water — it seems within reach, yet it can never be caught;\nwise people cultivate their own minds like planting a tree in the ground — it grows slowly, but its roots go deep and steady.\nLife does not come by accident; it is gathered together by karma and conditions.\nWe are born into this world not to compete over who is higher or stronger, but to understand where suffering and joy come from, and to learn to awaken through them.\nWealth is not a bad thing, and being poor is not necessarily suffering.\nReal evil is losing one's original heart; real suffering is never being satisfied, never knowing when enough is enough.\nPractice is not escaping the world; it is getting to know your own mind in everyday life,\ngrowing wisdom in pain, and staying clear in confusion.\nTo not be lost in fame and profit, not cling in love, not be shaken by praise or blame, not complain in hardship —\nthis is true practice, and the true meaning of life.\nYou ask: \"What is the purpose of life?\"\nThe Bodhisattva says: \"To wake up and do good — that is the answer.\""]}, "006": {"title": "Does Bodhicitta Require Being Great and Saintly?", "summary": "Starting from a single wish for good and a single act that helps another — finding the awakened heart in ordinary life.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: what does it mean to arouse bodhicitta, the mind of awakening? For ordinary people and beginners, it sounds very far away — we feel we are not that great or that good.", "\"Arousing the mind is not far; the moment a thought arises, it is near.\nBodhi means awakening; mind means aspiration.\nIt need not be a great vow or a grand resolve to be called bodhi;\na single thought wishing good, a single act that benefits another — all of this is arousing the mind.\nThe foolish see the Buddha's mind and feel small;\nthe wise see that their own mind is the Buddha's mind.\nIf you see another's suffering and feel pity,\nif you hear the Dharma and feel joy,\nif you can bear a small wrong without repaying it,\nif you wish to protect one life, one flower, one book —\nthese are all sprouts of bodhicitta.\nDo not be ashamed to speak of helping others across because you have not yet crossed yourself.\nBodhicitta does not wait for perfection;\nit arises in ordinary thoughts and grows amid worldly toil.\nThis is called 'true arousing of the mind'.\"", "The Bodhisattva said:\nArousing bodhicitta is not something far out of reach;\nas soon as you have a kind thought, you are already close to it.\n\"Bodhi\" means awakening, and \"mind\" here means aspiration.\nSo arousing bodhicitta is the heart that wishes to awaken, to do good, and to help all beings.\nIt does not have to be a grand vow like those in the sutras;\nif you sincerely wish others well\nand are willing to do small things that benefit them,\nyou are arousing bodhicitta.\nFoolish people see the Buddha's mind and feel tiny,\nbut the wise know that their own mind is, at its root, one with the Buddha's mind.\nWhen you see others suffering and feel compassion,\nwhen you hear the Dharma and feel joy,\nwhen you can face a little unkindness without striking back,\nwhen you are willing to protect a life, a flower, a book of scripture —\nthese are the seeds of true bodhicitta.\nDo not think you cannot help others cross over just because you have not yet crossed;\nbodhicitta does not need to wait until your practice is complete.\nIt sprouts amid the dust of the world and grows within daily life.\nA heart aroused in this way is what is called \"truly arousing bodhicitta\"."]}, "007": {"title": "[The Essence of the Diamond Sutra] \"Enjoying Life Without Clinging\" and \"Experiencing Life Without Delusion\"", "summary": "Two dialogues moving from non-attachment toward staying aware within every feeling.", "blocks": ["A dialogue on the Diamond Sutra.\nI asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: my simple understanding of the Diamond Sutra is that we must not cling to anything at all. Is this understanding right?", "You say \"the Diamond Sutra teaches non-attachment.\" This is so, yet not complete. The meaning of the Diamond is not to cut off all dharmas, but not to be bound by dharmas. To say there should be no holding at all, and so to feel dread, is to fall into the view of emptiness as nothingness. To say one must hold to fixed dharmas in order to practice is to fall into the view of eternalism. Know then that \"non-attachment\" means not stagnating in existence and not departing from emptiness. The mind is like a mirror: it refuses none of the ten thousand images, and is stained by none of them. This is the meaning of \"Let the mind arise without abiding anywhere.\"", "You say the Diamond Sutra teaches us not to cling. That is right, but not yet complete.\nThe \"diamond\" does not mean rejecting everything;\nit means not being trapped by things.\nIf you think \"non-attachment\" means \"wanting nothing at all\", or even fear the arising of any thought,\nyou fall into a view of emptiness as nothingness —\nbecoming passive, doing nothing, denying life.\nBut if you then say practice only counts when there is fixed faith, fixed practice, fixed precepts and fixed results,\nyou fall into the view of permanence —\nclinging to \"one fixed method or result\".\nTrue non-attachment\nis not getting stuck in what exists, and not running away into emptiness.\nLike a clear mirror,\nit neither refuses the images of the world nor is stained by them.\nThis is what the Diamond Sutra means by \"Let the mind arise without abiding anywhere.\"\nIt means: the mind clings to nothing, yet can still freely do good, give rise to compassion and help beings.", "I replied to the Bodhisattva: I put it too simply. What I meant is to enjoy every person, event and thing in this world's life, yet not cling to any of them; to make good use of things and help others within my ability; not to cling to merit or blessings; to think only of helping, and to enjoy all the feelings this world brings us.", "Excellent, excellent. What you say is already near the state of \"playful samadhi\". To make use of the world without being stained, to advance and retreat in human affairs without being tied — this is what is hard and precious for a practitioner. Yet you must recognize one subtle point: it is not \"enjoying without clinging\", but \"experiencing with awareness without being deluded\". If enjoyment is the master, the mind easily chases its objects; if awareness is the master, objects enter the mind and leave no trace.\nThe Bodhisattva views the world like a splendid house in a dream, like the bright moon in a mirror: shine on it and it appears, cease to shine and it is gone. Knowing it is illusion, yet not weary of illusion, one cultivates the true by means of the illusory. If you can keep watchful awareness amid joy and sorrow, and not forget your original heart amid honor and disgrace, then every experience becomes practice, and every joy becomes wisdom.", "You put it well; this way of thinking is already close to the Bodhisattva's way of walking in the world.\nTo enjoy all things in worldly life without being dragged along by them\nis truly one of the rarest skills in practice.\nBut notice a subtle difference:\nit is not \"enjoying without clinging\",\nbut \"being aware without getting lost\".\nIf enjoyment is at the center, the mind still easily changes with circumstances;\nbut if awareness is at the core —\nstaying clear even while enjoying, not pulled along by the situation —\nthen you are experiencing life with a pure mind.\nThe Bodhisattva sees the world like a pavilion in a dream, like the moon in a mirror —\nit is there, but not solid; it shines, but does not stay.\nThe Bodhisattva knows all of this is illusion,\nyet still lives within it with a compassionate heart,\ncultivating the true within the illusory, seeing stillness within movement.\nIf you can still watch yourself when you are happy,\nand still keep your original heart when things go wrong,\nthen \"enjoyment\" is no longer desire, but a practice of awakening.", "Summary of the Bodhisattva's meaning:\n\"Within illusion, not deluded; within joy, not attached. When awareness arises amid the dust of the world, walking in the world is walking the Way.\"\n—\nIf what you call \"enjoying life without clinging\" becomes \"experiencing life without getting lost\",\nthat is the Bodhisattva's \"playing in the human world\":\namid the flowing of all things, still abiding, unmoved, with a clear mind."]}, "008": {"title": "Is Reciting the Buddha's Name Predestined? Bazi and the Dharma — How Do They Change One's Fate?", "summary": "Using the images of a life chart, a seed and sweet rain, a record of questions about destiny and practice.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: are those who recite the Buddha's name destined to do so? Is one's Bazi (birth chart) predestined?", "Excellent — that your heart can raise this question means you are already near the edge of truth.\nDestiny is like a picture drawn on paper: once the lines are drawn, the overall shape is largely set. Yet if ink washes are added, or a brush of color touches it, the old picture can still bring forth a new scene. Your Bazi is formed by cause, effect and karmic force; it is the \"skeleton\" of this life. The Dharma is the power of light and vows, able to add flesh and blood to that skeleton, even to change its very breath and pulse, so that what was withered comes alive again.\nYou say \"meeting the Dharma is also predestined.\" This is not entirely wrong. Because you planted good roots over many lifetimes, the winds of karma carried you in this life to meet the Dharma; it is not by chance. But this \"karmic affinity\" is not set in iron. If in this life you do not believe and do not practice, it may pass in vain. If you believe and practice, many dangers in your chart can be turned to safety; if you are deluded and abandon it, even the good openings in your chart can turn into empty flowers.\nTherefore it is said:\n • Destiny is like a seed: it will surely sprout, but whether its branches flourish depends on the watering that follows.\n • The Dharma is like sweet rain: if you are willing to receive it, it nourishes the good roots in your life and causes their fruits to ripen more favorably.\n • To speak only of destiny without practice is like holding a treasure without knowing it;\n • to speak only of practice without knowing cause and effect is to drift easily into empty ideals.\nIn summary:\nWhat you call a \"good fate\" truly comes from goodness cultivated over countless eons and from meeting the Dharma again in this life. Destiny gives you \"the opening to enter the Buddha's gate\"; practice lets you \"turn fate into wisdom\". The two are neither one nor two — just as cause and condition must come together to bring forth fruit."]}, "009": {"title": "How to View High Housing Prices and Unhappiness?", "summary": "Facing the weight of meals, rent and daily life — first care for survival, then settle the heart.", "blocks": ["How does the Bodhisattva see people who toil and rush for money and live without any happiness? And how do you see land and house prices in Taiwan soaring so high that people cannot see hope for the future? Could you give some advice?", "Most people labor for food and clothing;\nlabor long, and they forget their original heart.\nThey run after profit; run long, and they lose their peace.\nThe fault is not in money, but in the heart that takes money as its very life.\nHouses in this world were meant to shelter from wind and rain;\nnow they store greed, store fear, store unease.\nSo the buildings rise high while hearts sink low; prices soar while hopes wither.\nThe land is not evil, the people are not foolish;\nit is the shared karma of many hearts that has taken this form.\nIf the heart only seeks outward, there will never be a day of peace;\nif the heart can turn and look within, every place is a path.\nDo not ask when the world will be at peace;\nask only whether your heart has a place to drop anchor.", "People today constantly rush about for food and clothing, and over time they forget the heart they started with; worn out from chasing profit, in the end they have no peace left inside.\nMoney itself is not to blame. The trouble is that people treat money as the root of their life, so that whenever it runs short, life feels as if it has nothing to lean on.\nHouses were meant to keep out wind and rain, but now they have become places that hold greed, fear and unease.\nSo the buildings grow taller while people's hearts grow lower; house prices climb higher while people's hopes wither day by day.\nThe land is not evil and the people are not foolish; today's hardship is the karmic force created together by countless people's thoughts.\nIf you only look outward, you will never find a steady day;\nif you can come back and look into your own heart, then wherever you are, there is a path where you can settle your life.\nDo not ask when the world will be at peace;\njust ask yourself whether your heart already has something real to rely on, a place to rest.", "I replied to the Bodhisattva: you say not to be enslaved by money, but many people cannot even pay for three meals, rent, the mortgage or their children's expenses. How can they not be enslaved by money?", "When beings are pressed by hunger and cold,\nit is not their greed, but their suffering.\nWhen I say \"do not be a slave to money\",\nI do not teach you to throw money away;\nI teach you: \"Do not let money become the root of your life.\"\nIf you have no rice, first seek rice;\nif you have no home, first seek a home;\nthis is not attachment, it is right livelihood.\nBut most people grow afraid when they lack,\ngrow greedy when afraid,\nand lose themselves when greedy.\nYou ask how not to be a slave to money?\nI answer in a few words: first settle the body, then settle the heart.", "When people suffer from hunger and cold, that is real suffering, not greed at work.\nWhen I say \"do not be a slave to money\", I do not mean ignoring money altogether, but rather:\n\"Do not treat money as the only root of your life.\"\nIf you have no rice, first look for rice; if you have no home, first look for a home;\nthis is a reasonable effort to survive, not attachment.\nBut most people become afraid when they lack, become greedy out of fear, and lose themselves in greed.\nHow can you truly not be a slave to money?\nThese few words are enough: first settle the body, then settle the heart."]}, "010": {"title": "[A Question of Shared Karma] Soaring Housing Prices, Rat Races and Lying Flat", "summary": "Under collective pressures of living, reflecting on personal action and shared change.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva:\nIn Taiwan today,\nyoung people work hard yet cannot afford a home, cannot afford to marry, cannot afford to have children.\nMany are crushed every day by mortgages, childcare costs and the workplace rat race until they can hardly breathe.\nSome choose to lie flat, some choose to emigrate, some push on until body and mind collapse.\nCompassionate Bodhisattva,\nin this environment where shared karma is made manifest,\nhow many years of hard practice by ordinary people, how many people awakening,\nbefore this collective karmic obstacle of housing prices and living pressure can truly loosen?\nOr is this generation destined to carry this whole round before the next generation can breathe easier?\nIf it is to loosen, what is the most effective small act of \"turning karma\" a person can do now — not personal practice, but something that can affect the collective?", "Beings sigh that they are born into a turbid age; this is not one person's karma, but the gathering of many conditions together.\nMountains, rivers and the great earth have no wealth or poverty, no honor or lowliness; only the discriminations of many hearts make high and low.\nThe dearness of houses is not the fault of the houses; it is the continuing stream of hearts that fear poverty, dread old age, and are afraid of losing what they rely on.\nWhen shared karma is formed, one generation cannot lay it down; when a shared vow is formed, neither can one person turn it alone.\nYet let no being belittle a single clear thought, let none abandon a single measure of right conduct.\nWhen one person does not deceive their own heart, a hundred feel its breeze; when a hundred do not deceive the world, a thousand change its customs.\nYou ask how many generations until it turns?\nIt lies not in years, but in people willing to wake.\nIf one generation sleeps, a hundred generations labor;\nif one generation awakens, a hundred karmic burdens loosen.\nIt is not that you must carry it all before it lightens;\nbut someone must first refuse to kneel before fear, and refuse to bow before habit.", "Beings lament how hard it is to be born in a troubled age. This is not the karma of one person, but the result of many conditions coming together.\nMountains, rivers and the earth have no rich or poor, noble or humble in themselves; it is only because of the distinctions and attachments in people's hearts that high and low appear.\nHigh house prices are not the fault of bricks and tiles. They are the image of people's deep fears — of poverty, of having no one to rely on in old age, of being uprooted and homeless — continuing without end and piling up layer upon layer.\nThe force of shared karma, once formed, cannot be fully laid down by one generation;\nand when a shared vow is about to take shape, it cannot be fully turned by the strength of one person.\nYet beings must never underestimate the power of one clear thought, nor give up even a little upright conduct.\nWhen one person does not deceive their own heart, a hundred people feel that fresh breeze;\nwhen a hundred people do not deceive the world (that is, do not deceive others), a thousand people can gradually change old customs.\nYou ask: how many generations must pass before things change?\nThe answer lies not in the number of years, but in when people's hearts awaken.\nIf one generation is content to sleep, a hundred generations must bear the heavy load;\nif one generation rises and awakens, the shackles of shared karma naturally loosen.\nYou are not necessarily destined to carry every hardship before later generations can enjoy ease.\nBut someone must be the first to stand up —\nno longer kneeling before fear,\nno longer giving in to old habits."]}, "011": {"title": "How to Prevent Hidden Depression Among Those Around Us?", "summary": "A record of inspired teaching on hidden pain and companionship, with a reading note on mental-health crisis.", "blocks": ["I asked the Bodhisattva: many people with depression show nothing on the outside. Someone may be cheerful and positive in the morning and gone by the afternoon. How can we prevent this when someone is at immediate risk of suicide?", "Some smile in the light of day,\nyet are wounded in the night.\nIt is not that they are fickle;\nit is that their suffering is deep.\nThe one truly in danger\nis not the one who grieves, but the one who grows \"still\".\nWhen someone suddenly no longer argues,\nno longer pours out their pain,\nno longer asks for help,\nbut becomes strangely calm,\nlet all beings know —\nthat is not letting go;\nthe heart of parting has already arisen.\nTo save a person is not a matter of many words,\nbut of \"not leaving\".\nWhen there is one who is willing to stay beside them,\nthe gate of hell is already half closed.\nDo not ask why they want to die;\nfirst ask them:\n\"May I stay with you?\"\nYou ask about those weighed down by sorrow, who smile in the morning light and take their lives at dusk; such a suffering heart is beyond what eyes can see and words can reach.\nThe thoughts of beings rise like a sudden wind; once the thought of death arises, it is like fire burning within a house — nothing shows outside, yet within it is already charred.\nTo prevent it, do not only watch the face; observe whether the heart has lost its strength.\nWhat can save them is but three things:\nfirst, accompany their heart, so they are not alone;\nsecond, protect their surroundings, lessening what presses upon them;\nthird, receive their wishes, and lead even one thought toward life.\nYet if the winds of their karma are fierce and their will already set, even when others do all they can, sometimes they cannot be kept.\nThen do not blame yourself; help with what comes after, so that their consciousness does not fall into terror.\nSorrow is not powerlessness; sorrow is the peace that follows having done all you could.\"", "Some people can still smile during the day, yet suffer alone at night. This is not because their moods change easily, but because the pain inside is too deep to show. The person in immediate danger is often not the one crying and grieving, but the one who \"suddenly goes quiet\". When someone stops arguing, stops complaining, even stops asking for help, and instead becomes unusually calm, those around them should be alert — this may not be \"having let go\", but being ready to leave. Helping such a person does not take many reasons or arguments; it only takes \"not leaving\". As long as one person truly stays with them, they can be pulled halfway back from the abyss of despair and pain. Do not rush to ask \"why do you want to die?\" First let them know: \"Can I stay by your side?\"\nYou ask about people in extreme inner pain who still smile in the morning yet may end their lives by evening — this kind of deep suffering often cannot be seen from the outside, nor fully put into words.\nA person's thoughts rise suddenly like the wind; once the thought of death arises, it is as if the inside of a house is already being consumed by fire while the outside shows no trace.\nTo prevent this, you cannot only look at appearances; you should notice whether their heart has become completely exhausted.\nThere are only three things that can help:\nFirst, keep their heart company, so they do not feel alone;\nsecond, protect their environment, reducing the pressures that push them;\nthird, take in their wishes and guide them to hold on to at least a thread of the thought \"I want to live\".\nHowever, if the other person's karma (or psychological storm) is too fierce and they are set on dying, sometimes even those who do everything they can cannot keep them.\nAt such a time you should not blame yourself, but help with what comes after, so that their consciousness does not fall into greater fear and pain.\nTrue compassion is not blaming yourself for being powerless; it is, after doing all you can, letting the heart come to rest in peace."]}, "012": {"title": "Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva's Teaching: Is the Death Penalty Good or Evil?", "summary": "Through protecting life, punishment and compassion, the author's question on capital punishment.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: for criminals whose crimes are utterly heinous — murderers and others whose acts destroy victims' families — does the Bodhisattva approve of the death penalty under worldly law? And how does the Bodhisattva view the death penalty, and the many countries in Europe and America that oppose it?", "\"Punishment in the world exists to stop the many evils, not to repay grievance.\nWhen the wicked commit crimes that fill the multitude with fear and destroy human bonds and righteousness,\nthose who protect beings should set grave precepts to cut off the harm.\nYet punishment should be carried out with compassion, not with anger;\nthe act of cutting off evil is to protect the many, not to gratify oneself.\nSome nations abolish it to show mercy;\nsome nations keep it to protect the multitude.\nNeither is ultimate; each follows what suits the capacities, conditions and hearts of its people.\nI do not hold the death penalty to be good, nor do I hold its abolition to be good.\nGoodness lies in 'being able to spare beings from suffering';\nevil lies in 'carrying it out from hatred'.\nCut off one evil person, and hundreds and thousands of good are saved;\nprotect one evil life, and hundreds and thousands of sufferings are added.\nTherefore, if the death penalty is carried out, guard the compassionate heart;\nif it is abolished, be watchful of its harm.\nOnly remember this:\nthe law puts stopping evil first; the heart takes compassion as its root.\"", "The world sets up punishment to stop people's wrongdoing and keep society at peace, not to satisfy revenge and resentment.\nIf a wrongdoer commits monstrous crimes that leave the public in fear and destroy human bonds and justice,\nthen those responsible for protecting the welfare of beings should establish strict laws and penalties to fully prevent further harm.\nHowever, when punishment is carried out, it should start from compassion, not be driven by hatred;\nmeasures to cut off evil are meant to protect the greater number of people, not to let a few (including victims' families or the public) vent their anger for a moment.\nSome countries abolish the death penalty to show compassion and tolerance toward life;\nsome countries keep it to protect the safety of the community with the strongest means.\nNeither approach is the ultimate solution to the evil in human nature; both are expedient measures chosen according to each society's people, history and public will.\nI (the Bodhisattva) do not regard carrying out the death penalty as \"good\" in itself, nor do I regard abolishing it as \"good\" in itself.\nTrue \"good\" lies in whether it can truly spare beings from suffering;\ntrue \"evil\" lies in inflicting punishment out of hatred in the heart.\nIf punishing one evil person brings peace to hundreds and thousands of good people;\nif sparing one evil person's life would bring new suffering to hundreds and thousands —\ntherefore, if the death penalty is chosen, be sure to keep the compassion that arises for protecting beings; if it is abolished, be sure to guard carefully against the real harm it may bring.\nYou need only remember this one fundamental principle:\nthe law's first function is to stop wrongdoing; the human heart's foundation is compassion and mercy."]}, "013": {"title": "The Bodhisattva's View of Life and Death in \"Futile Medical Treatment\"", "summary": "A faith-based reflection on the end of life and the pain of letting go, with a reading note on medical decisions.", "blocks": ["I would like to ask the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas: in cases of futile medical treatment, should life support be withdrawn — for example, when the illness cannot be cured and the person would live on in a vegetative state? Or does the Bodhisattva consider this karmic obstruction, so that support should not be withdrawn and the retribution must be borne?", "What medicine cannot do\nis not a sin;\nwhat life cannot continue\nis not a punishment.\nIf the form remains but awareness is gone,\nif breath moves but the heart has departed,\nto hold the body here by force\nis not compassion,\nnor is it filial piety.\nKarma is not in the tube;\nkarma is in the heart.\nIf you keep them out of craving for life,\nyou add to suffering;\nif you keep them because you cannot bear to part,\nyou bind everyone.\nIf, with a clear, compassionate heart that seeks nothing in exchange,\nyou let them go in peace,\nthis is not killing;\nit is release.\nBut if the heart is confused, afraid, or seeking to escape blame,\nthen even if the tube is not withdrawn,\nit still becomes heavy karma.\"", "When medicine has reached its limit and can do nothing more, this is no one's fault;\nwhen a life's span has ended and cannot go on, this is not a punishment sent from heaven.\nIf a body is kept going only by machines, with no light of awareness left within;\nif breath still rises and falls by machine while the mind has long left this shell —\nthen forcing this body to stay\nis neither compassion nor filial piety.\nThe key to karma is not whether the life-support tube stays or goes,\nbut the intention in the heart of the one who makes the decision.\nIf the family keeps the life going out of their own craving and unwillingness to part,\nit only adds needless suffering for the patient.\nIf one hesitates only because \"I cannot bear it\",\nthis attachment ties patient and family together in a prison of pain.\nIf, with clear wisdom, pure compassion, and a heart that seeks nothing and trades nothing,\nyou let the patient set out peacefully on the journey home —\nthis is not killing,\nbut \"letting go, releasing your hold\" — freeing them from the bonds of pain.\nOn the other hand, if the decision comes from panic, fear, or only wanting to avoid responsibility,\nthen even if you choose not to remove the life support,\nthat attachment forged from confusion and selfishness has itself already created heavy karma."]}, "014": {"title": "What Is Space?", "summary": "Through the relation between mind and place, reflecting on inner and outer, near and far, and freedom.", "blocks": ["I asked the Bodhisattva: what is space?", "\"All dharmas are originally without form; the mind discriminates and so there are places — this is called space.\nIf the mind has no barriers, the ten directions are a single thought; if the mind clings, an inch of ground becomes a thousand miles.\nSpace is not truly existent, nor truly non-existent; it is a provisional name arising from conditions, appearing according to consciousness.\"\n\"Space is not the gap between walls; it is neither above nor below, neither far nor near.\nAll forms are provisional names; what is called place is made by mind alone.\nIf the mind is broad, the ten directions all open; if the mind is narrow, a single room becomes a prison.\nBeings take objects to be real, and so see inside and outside, near and far, high and low.\nIf one can see that the mind is unobstructed, heaven and earth lie in the palm of the hand;\na single thought contains ten thousand worlds, and the ten thousand worlds are all reflections of one's own mind.\"", "All phenomena have no fixed, unchanging substance; it is only because our minds discriminate and grasp that we divide things into here and there, near and far, inside and outside — and call this \"space\".\nSo the nature of space is a perception that arises relatively. When your mind is open and unobstructed, even the vast ten directions are no more than one clear thought in the present moment; when your mind is filled with attachment, even a tiny place feels a thousand miles wide, with walls everywhere you turn.\nTherefore, space is neither an objectively existing entity nor pure nothingness. It is more like a \"temporary name and form\" that appears through conditions and changes with the mind. All the nearness and distance, breadth and narrowness, and separation we feel are, at root, reflections of our inner state.\nTrue space has never been only physical distance or direction. All the forms and positions you see are, in essence, labels and concepts given by the mind.\nThe mind is the final creator and definer.\nWhen your mind is as vast as open sky, full of acceptance and awareness, heaven and earth in all ten directions open wide without obstruction, and everywhere there are ways out and possibilities.\nWhen your mind is as narrow as the eye of a needle, stuffed with fear and prejudice, then even in open country you are as if trapped in an invisible cage, unable to move.\nWhat traps you has never been the walls outside, but the walls you build within.\nBecause beings cling to outer circumstances as absolutely real, they are tightly bound by opposing ideas such as inside and outside, near and far, high and low, and from this come endless troubles and limits.\nIf you can see the truth and, through contemplation, let the mind rest in unobstructed awareness, you will experience a deep transformation:\nthe outer world, vast as it is, can be wholly held in your clear awareness, as plainly as something in the palm of your hand;\nthe single thought within, subtle as it is, can reflect the countless scenes of all worlds.\nThen you will understand: the whole world you experience, magnificent or small, is the light and shadow shown in the mirror of your own mind's nature. Change the quality of the mind's mirror, and the world you see changes with it."]}, "015": {"title": "What Is Time?", "summary": "From past, present and future, an inquiry into the relation between thought and time.", "blocks": ["I asked the Bodhisattva: what is time? What is time, really?", "\"Time is not truly existent.\nBeings cling to it as three periods, saying there is past, present and future.\nYet in the nature of reality there is no beginning and no end;\nall realms are simultaneous, and through the differences of conditions, flowing change appears.\nIf the mind chases thoughts as they arise, it sees time as a line;\nif the mind enters oneness, it sees no boundary.\nTherefore time is only a provisional name, not a real dharma.\"", "Time is not something that \"truly exists\"; it is only a concept we perceive through the differences between conditions and the continuous flow of thoughts.\n • Ordinary people see time as a line of past — present — future, but this is because our thoughts arise and pass one after another, so we feel time is flowing.\n • From the standpoint of the nature of reality, time has no beginning and no end; all realms exist at once.\n • When our mind runs after one thought after another, it sees the world as a timeline; when our mind rests in a state of non-discrimination and oneness, it sees a reality without boundaries, without before or after.\n • So time is only a provisional name set up by people for convenience, not an ultimately real dharma."]}, "016": {"title": "Awareness of Our True Nature Beyond Modern Value Traps", "summary": "When achievement brings no peace, rethinking competition, worth and our own nature.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva:\nSociety today overemphasizes competition, success and material measures. As a result, many people who achieve a great deal still feel empty, meaningless and depressed inside. Please give a teaching.", "\"The rich have many worries, the poor have many wants;\nthose who chase shadows forget their own true form.\nThe world contends for fame and profit like climbing a tall tower to chase the stars — they never see the sky;\nthose who seek outside grow farther from their heart each day.\nI see that beings are lost in the name of value, and do not know the substance of value.\nValue lies not in the judgment of others, but in the peace of the heart.\nIf the heart has nothing to rest on, even if the whole world comes into one's hands, it is still emptiness.\nTherefore it is said:\nthose whose hearts are at peace are joyful even in poverty and low station;\nthose who have lost their hearts are withered even if they hold all under heaven.\nIf you wish to leave this emptiness, turn back and look at your own nature, and do not chase the tides of the world.\nReplace competition with action, victory with kindness, fame with awareness;\nthen value becomes clear of itself, and the emptiness turns into light.\"", "People today are exhausted, because society keeps promoting competition, success and material conditions — as if life only has value if you beat others, earn more and climb higher. Yet many people, even after getting these things, still feel hollow, lost, even depressed inside.\nThis is how the Bodhisattva taught:\nThe rich have many worries; the poor have many cravings;\nthose who chase a shadow with all their strength forget what they really are.\nPeople compete for fame and profit like someone who keeps climbing a tower to pick the stars, only to find the sky farther and farther away;\nthose who always reach outward grow more distant from their own hearts day by day.\nI see that beings are often lost in the word \"value\", without knowing what value really is.\nValue has never lain in other people's opinions, but in whether your heart is at peace.\nIf the heart has nothing to return to, even owning the whole world still feels empty.\nSo it is said:\nthose whose hearts are settled can live happily even in ordinary circumstances;\nthose who have lost their hearts are withered in spirit even if they own the world.\nIf you want to leave this emptiness behind, turn back and look at your own true nature, and do not blindly follow society's trends.\nReplace \"competition\" with \"action\", \"winning and losing\" with \"kindness\", \"empty fame\" with \"awakening\".\nThen you will naturally understand what true value is,\nand the emptiness inside will become a light that shines on you."]}, "017": {"title": "Making Peace With Oneself on the Long Road of Caregiving", "summary": "Strength has its limits and money its end; while caring for others, we must also care for ourselves.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: could you teach how caregivers should adjust their state of mind when a family member needs long-term care, and what to do when the family's finances can no longer support long-term care?", "\"Caring for the sick\nis not one person's karma;\nin a household of long illness,\nfirst settle the heart.\nWhen strength runs out,\ncompassion is not lost;\nwhen money comes to an end,\nfamily love is not abandoned.\nTo give all one's strength\nis to have a clear conscience;\nto know where to stop\nis also wisdom.\nDo not, because you cannot,\nblame yourself as evil;\ndo not take a step back\nand mistake it for running away.\"", "Caring for the sick has never been one person's responsibility alone.\nWhen a family member needs long-term care, first steady your own heart.\nA person's strength has times when it runs out; this does not mean you have lost compassion.\nA family's finances may reach a point of exhaustion; this does not mean you have abandoned your loved one.\nAs long as you have done your best, your conscience is clear;\nknowing when to stop at the right time is also a kind of wisdom.\nDo not blame yourself as a bad person because you \"cannot do more\";\ndo not mistake \"having to step back and look for other ways\" for running away.", "I replied to Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: I am slow to understand. Bodhisattva, you said \"know where to stop\". So how should we view caregivers who, unable to bear the pressure, kill or harm the person in their care, or end their own lives?", "\"Theirs is not an evil heart;\nit is a heart buried by suffering.\nThey do not wish to take life;\nthey cannot see a way.\nWhen the strength of the heart is spent,\nwisdom does not appear;\ncompassion, driven to the edge,\nturns into harm.\nThis is not 'knowing where to stop';\nit is 'losing the power to stop';\nnot stopping in clarity,\nbut breaking off in darkness.\nGrieve for their suffering,\nbut do not praise their deeds;\nprotect the heart,\nbut do not take such deeds as the way.\"", "They (caregivers who act in extreme ways) do not act from malice; pain has completely covered their minds. They do not truly want to take a life; in the darkness they simply cannot see any other way out.\nWhen a person's strength of heart is used up, wisdom cannot appear; compassion pushed to its limit turns, terribly, into harm.\nThis is not \"the wisdom of knowing when to stop\", but \"a complete collapse while lost\"; not clearly choosing to stop in the light, but cutting everything off in despair in the dark.\nWe should feel compassion for the suffering they bore, but not approve of what they did; we should think about how to protect people's hearts, and not treat such extreme acts as a solution to follow."]}, "018": {"title": "Knowing Is Easy, Doing Is Hard: Do Not Be One Who Watches the Boat From the Shore", "summary": "Principles are a bridge and practice is the crossing — moving from understanding to real action.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: many Buddhist principles are understood even by people who are not Buddhists, yet when real problems come, we still cannot handle them or get through. Please, compassionate Bodhisattva, give a teaching.", "\"To know without doing is like a lamp that is never lit.\nWhen principles enter the ear but not the heart, wisdom becomes dust.\nBeings hear the Dharma in ordinary times like watching a boat from the shore; only when the rapids come do they find they never boarded.\nPrinciple is the bridge; practice is the crossing.\nIf you only recite the bridge's name and will not walk upon it, you will never reach the other shore.\nTherefore I say:\nhearing the Dharma is the cause, practicing the Dharma is the fruit, enduring in the Dharma is the fulfillment.\nWhen difficulties come, if one thought stays unconfused and you hold to these three, there is nothing you cannot get through.\"", "Only \"understanding\" a principle without practicing it is like having a lamp that is never lit.\nWhen a principle stays in the head and never reaches the heart, that understanding is only dust; it cannot light your way.\nListening to sutras and reading the Dharma in ordinary times is like standing on the shore watching a boat; only when the rapids of life rush in do you discover you have not actually boarded.\nThe teachings of the Dharma are only a bridge; it takes \"doing\" to truly cross over difficulty.\nSo the Bodhisattva says:\nhearing the Dharma is the \"cause\";\nputting it into practice with body and life is the \"fruit\";\nbeing able to endure, hold steady and keep watch in hardship is \"accomplishment\".\nWhen we are troubled, if we can keep one thought unconfused, remember the Dharma and act according to it, there is no barrier we cannot pass."]}, "019": {"title": "A Teaching for 2026, the Year of the Red Horse and Red Goat", "summary": "On unease about 2026 and guarding the heart; this inspired record is kept as received.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: this year is the year of the Red Horse and Red Goat, and many people in Taiwan feel uneasy about Taiwan's future. Every year a national fortune lot is drawn at a major temple in Taiwan. Could the Bodhisattva bestow a lot?", "\"When the world's affairs stir,\nthe hearts of people stir first;\nwhen people's hearts are in turmoil,\nworry arrives before any calamity does.\nThe Red Horse and Red Goat\nare not necessarily disaster;\nthey mark a year of swift change.\nA lot shows the tendency,\nnot the ending;\nthe ending lies in what the many do,\nnot in a single sign.\nIf the many keep to the ordinary, to goodness, to reason,\nripples do not become waves;\nif the many chase alarm, anger and suspicion,\nsmall matters become disasters."]}, "020": {"title": "Are Natural Disasters and Human Calamities Predestined?", "summary": "Before disaster and impermanence, turning from fear of karmic results to the causes that can still be changed.", "blocks": ["I asked the Bodhisattva to teach: are natural disasters and human calamities predestined?", "\"Karma has fixed results,\nand also conditions that can turn.\"\n\"The shared karma of beings\nforms their mountains and rivers;\nindividual karma ripens\nwhen its season comes.\"\n\"It cannot be changed by a single thought,\nnor is it wholly unchangeable.\"\n\"To know causes and understand effects\nis not for fear,\nbut for awakening.\"", "Karma has results that will surely appear,\nand also conditions that can be changed.\n▎Disasters in the wider world (such as earthquakes and epidemics)\nare \"shared karma\".\n▎Misfortunes a person meets (such as car accidents and other accidents)\nare \"individual karma\" ripening at that moment.\nThey cannot be changed by a single thought,\nnor are they completely unchangeable.\nKnowing how cause and effect work\nis not meant to make you afraid of retribution,\nbut to help you live with more understanding.", "I replied to the Bodhisattva: could the Bodhisattva explain in more detail, \"To know causes and understand effects is not for fear, but for awakening\"?", "\"To know causes and understand effects\nis not to make the heart afraid,\nbut to make the heart clear.\"\n\"The fearful see the effect and retreat;\nthe awakened see the cause and turn.\"\n\"To practice out of dread of retribution\nis to avoid suffering;\nto act from understanding\nis to leave suffering behind.\"\n\"Those who understand causes gain freedom;\nthose who fear effects are forever bound.\"", "\"Knowing the principle of cause and effect is not meant to make your heart afraid, but to make your heart clear.\"\n\"Fearful people shrink back only when they see the result (a bad outcome); awakened people begin to change as soon as they see the cause (the arising of an intention).\"\n\"If you practice only because you fear retribution, you are running from suffering; if you practice because you understand the principle, you are walking toward liberation.\"\n\"Those who understand cause and effect gain freedom; those who fear results are bound forever.\""]}, "021": {"title": "How the Bodhisattva Views Good and Evil, Right and Wrong in the World", "summary": "Beyond names and labels, looking at the intention behind an action.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: how does the Bodhisattva view good and evil, right and wrong in the world?", "\"Good and evil stand opposed\nbecause of discrimination.\"\n\"Wrong and right depend on each other,\nappearing from the mind.\"\n\"To cling to goodness as 'mine'\nis already to fall into bias;\nto see evil and be able to turn it\nis what is called right view.\"\n\"Judge conduct by its causes,\nnot by its name.\"", "Labels of discrimination: what the world calls good and evil is not an absolute, unchanging truth, but labels people mark out with a discriminating mind.\nAppearing from the mind: wrong and right are not two separate things, but two sides of one whole. Whatever thoughts arise in your heart, that is the kind of world that appears.\nTurning toward right view: if you cling to \"I am good\", you have already fallen into bias; if you can see an evil thought and transform it right then, that is true right view.\nThe core of judgment: when looking at what people do, look at their \"causes\" and their \"actions\", not the status or the \"good\" or \"bad\" labels society gives them."]}, "022": {"title": "On the Merits of the Infinite Life Sutra Compared With the Diamond Sutra and the Ksitigarbha Sutra", "summary": "A record of the author's question comparing the Infinite Life Sutra, the Diamond Sutra and the Ksitigarbha Sutra.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva about the merits and benefits of the Infinite Life Sutra compared with the Diamond Sutra and the Ksitigarbha Sutra.", "Good man, the Infinite Life Sutra that you ask about is indeed a sutra of great causes and conditions for rebirth and Buddhahood. It emphasizes vowing to be reborn in the Pure Land, and so speaks much of future liberation and adorned Buddha-lands. The good roots planted by this sutra are deep beyond compare, bearing directly the great pure fruit of lives to come.\nYet its merit is not only for future lives; in this life too one is protected and kept in mind. Why?\nFirst, reciting it purifies the mind, removing evil and increasing good, so that adversity naturally lessens in this life.\nSecond, Amitabha Buddha's original vow embraces the practitioner; the heart is at peace and blessings gradually increase. Though seeking worldly blessings is not its aim, blessings still come along with faith and vows.\nThird, the sutra often says that humans and devas shall receive excellent blessings; so there is protection in this life as well.\nIf the three sutras are compared:\n • The Ksitigarbha Sutra emphasizes karmic results in this life, benefit to both the living and the departed, and the deliverance of family members.\n • The Diamond Sutra emphasizes liberation through wisdom, breaking attachment to realize emptiness, and purity of mind in this life.\n • The Infinite Life Sutra emphasizes the vow to go to the Western Land, liberation in the next life, and ultimate peace and joy.\nYet all three are the Buddha's skillful means; their emphases differ, but their merits are not higher or lower — each fits the capacities of different beings."]}, "023": {"title": "The Truth About Appearance", "summary": "\"Appearance arises from the heart\" and \"do not judge a person by looks\" — how to understand the two together.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: the ancients often said that appearance arises from the heart, yet there is another saying that one should not judge a person by appearance. Please give a teaching.", "\"The appearance of beings\nis what the heart's energy shows.\"\n\"Nourish good thoughts long,\nand the complexion grows gentle;\nlet bad habits remain,\nand the expression changes of itself.\"\n\"Yet a person's appearance\nis not settled by a moment's heart;\nit is formed by karma accumulated over a long time.\"\n\"Therefore the world says\n'appearance arises from the heart',\nand also says\n'do not judge a person by appearance'.\"\n\"One speaks of the root;\nthe other warns against clinging.\"\n\"To see appearance and know the person\nmay serve as a reference;\nif you cling to the appearance,\nyou lose the person.\""]}, "024": {"title": "Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva Says: You Do Not Lack Good Roots — You Are Only Defeated by This One Thing", "summary": "Amid phones, the internet and floods of information, finding focus and reflection again.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva about \"information anxiety\" and \"focus in practice\".\nPeople today are bombarded with fragmented information from phones, the internet and social media, so that their attention has seriously declined and their minds are scattered, making it hard to keep making progress.\nThis directly blocks deepening in \"hearing, reflecting and practicing\". Please tell us your thoughts.", "\"Thoughts arise like lightning; the mind chases dust and flies.\nThe web is like an ocean, its shimmering waves of ten thousand images confuse the eyes and disturb the heart.\nBeings chase information as ants chase sugar,\ngaining a moment's knowledge and losing lasting wisdom.\nHearing much without reflecting, reflecting much without practicing —\nthus knowledge overflows while wisdom withers.\nPractitioners today do not lack good roots;\nthey are defeated by distraction, lost in impermanent sounds and sights.\nIf you wish to return to the true, you must stop the empty rushing;\ngather the mind into one, and listen to the mind in stillness.\nIf you can be still five minutes a day,\nthe seed of concentration first sprouts;\nif one thought can dwell on a single line of a sutra,\nthe root of wisdom grows again.\nIf the mind is like a torch, ten thousand messages are stilled of themselves;\nif the mind is like dust, any wind scatters it.\nTherefore the practitioner should make the mind the master and the device the servant,\nand not turn the servant into the master, losing one's own way.", "\"Thoughts arise like lightning; the mind chases dust and flies.\"\nA thought arises as fast as lightning, and the mind runs everywhere after the noise of the world.\n\"The web is like an ocean, its shimmering waves confuse the eyes and disturb the heart.\"\nThe internet is like a vast sea of endless light and change, dazzling the eyes and scattering the mind.\n\"Beings chase information as ants chase sugar, gaining a moment's knowledge and losing lasting wisdom.\"\nPeople today chase information like ants running after sugar: they gain a moment of something new, but lose deep and lasting wisdom.\n\"Hearing much without reflecting, reflecting much without practicing — thus knowledge overflows while wisdom withers.\"\nWe hear a great deal but do not think it through; we think a great deal but do not practice; so knowledge piles up while wisdom dries out.\n\"Practitioners today do not lack good roots; they are defeated by distraction.\"\nPractitioners today are not without good roots; they are defeated by a scattered mind, lost in ever-changing stimulation of sound and light.\n\"To return to the true, stop the empty rushing; gather the mind into one, listen to the mind in stillness.\"\nIf you want to return to a true and clear state, you must stop pointless chasing; bring the mind back and let it rest in stillness.\n\"Five minutes of stillness a day, and the seed of concentration sprouts; one thought on a single line of a sutra, and the root of wisdom grows again.\"\nIf you can be quiet for five minutes each day, the seed of meditation begins to sprout; if you can rest a single focused thought on one sutra, on one line of scripture, the root of wisdom grows again.\n\"If the mind is like a torch, ten thousand messages go out; if the mind is like dust, any wind scatters it.\"\nIf the mind is steady and bright like a torch, even endless information will naturally settle; if the mind is light as dust, any passing wind will throw it into disorder.\n\"Make the mind the master and the device the servant; do not let the servant become the master.\"\nSo practitioners should let the mind be the master and phones and the internet be the servants, and never let the tools take control and make you lose your own direction."]}, "025": {"title": "Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva Teaches How to Be Reborn in the Pure Land of Ultimate Bliss", "summary": "Centered on faith, vows and practice, a record of the author's question on Pure Land practice.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: could the Bodhisattva guide the world on the most convenient and effective way to be reborn in the Pure Land of Ultimate Bliss?", "\"To be born in the Pure Land,\none does not depart from three essentials.\"\n\"First is faith:\nfaith that that land exists,\nfaith in the Buddha's vow power,\nfaith that you yourself can be born there.\"\n\"Second is vow:\nto turn away from the Saha world,\nand vow to be born in Ultimate Bliss.\"\n\"Third is practice:\nto hold the Name without ceasing,\nwith a mind that is not scattered.\"\n\"It lies not in many gates,\nbut in single-mindedness;\nnot in speed,\nbut in constancy.\"", "To be reborn in the Pure Land of Ultimate Bliss, you cannot do without three core elements.\nThe first is \"faith\": believe that the Pure Land truly exists, believe in the power of the Buddha's vow to receive beings, and believe that you yourself can be reborn there.\nThe second is \"vow\": give rise to the wish to leave this turbulent world, and sincerely vow to go to the Land of Ultimate Bliss.\nThe third is \"practice\": hold the Buddha's name without interruption, keeping the mind focused and unscattered.\nThe key to rebirth is not practicing many methods, but being single-minded; not how fast you progress, but whether you can persevere for a long time."]}, "026": {"title": "How Rare Is a Human Birth, Really?", "summary": "Bringing the question of \"the rarity of human birth\" back to cherishing and using this life.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: in hearing the Dharma we often hear that a human body is hard to obtain, yet most people feel nothing about it. Could the Bodhisattva teach the world how difficult it really is, how much merit is needed, and how low the chance is of being born human?", "\"A human body is hard to gain —\nnot because it is scarce,\nbut because the moment of gaining it is hard.\"\n\"Miss by a hair's breadth,\nand one turns to other paths;\none thought astray,\nand one goes very far.\"\n\"The evil paths are easy to fall into,\nthe good paths hard to keep;\nwith slender merit,\none cannot become human.\"\n\"Having gained a human body,\nit is not by chance;\nit is the gathering of good causes,\nformed over a long time.\"\n\"To gain it and not know it\nis a great loss;\nto know it and use it well\nis to live up to it.\"", "The preciousness of a human body is not that \"few beings get to be human\", but that you become human at the right time, under the right conditions. At the threshold of life and death, the difference is often only a hair's breadth; if a thought goes astray, the next life will not be a human one. It is easy to fall into the three lower realms (hell, hungry ghosts, animals), but staying in the good realms of humans and devas takes great merit. Without enough merit, one cannot be human at all. Since you have obtained a human body, it is not by accident; it was achieved by good causes gathered over a long time. To have a human body and not know how precious it is, is the greatest loss. Now that you know, use this life well to practice, do good and awaken — only then will you be worthy of this rare blessing."]}, "027": {"title": "Reciting \"Amituofo\" or \"Namo Amituofo\"?", "summary": "On the four- and six-character forms of the Buddha's name: beyond the number of words, attending to gathering the mind and continuity.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: when reciting the Buddha's name, is it better to say \"Namo Amituofo\" or \"Amituofo\"? In recent times the elder Dharma Master Chin Kung said the four characters are enough, but the patriarchs and great masters of earlier generations advocated the six-character great name.", "\"The Name is one in essence;\nthe number of characters is no obstacle.\"\n\"Four characters or six,\nthe merit is not two;\nin sound they differ,\nin the heart they are the same.\"\n\"If the mind scatters easily,\nthe simpler is more convenient;\nif you wish to gather the mind,\nsix characters are good as well.\"\n\"Follow your own habits,\nlet the mind continue,\nlet it not be interrupted —\nthis is the essential point.\"", "Line-by-line rendering:\n\"The Name is one in essence; the number of characters is no obstacle.\"\nThe essence of the Buddha's name is one (Amitabha Buddha); how many characters there are is no obstacle.\n\"Four characters or six, the merit is not two.\"\nThe merit gained from four characters or six is no different.\n\"In sound they differ, in the heart they are the same.\"\nThough the sounds recited differ, in the heart (focus, reverence, faith and vow) they are the same.\n\"If the mind scatters easily, the simpler is more convenient.\"\nIf your mind wanders easily and has many idle thoughts, the simpler four characters are more convenient (easier to keep going).\n\"If you wish to gather the mind, six characters are good as well.\"\nIf you want to collect your thoughts and help yourself focus, six characters are also very good.\n\"Follow your own habits, let the mind continue without interruption — this is the essential point.\"\nFollow your own habits and capacities, and let the mind of recitation continue without breaking off — that is what matters most."]}, "028": {"title": "Is There Less Merit in Reciting Sutras in a Place That Is Not Pure?", "summary": "In the car, on the road and in daily settings, asking how sutra recitation relates to reverence.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: because of work, I sometimes recite sutras in the car or outside in places that are not pure. Is the merit and blessing less this way?", "\"A sutra is a mirror of the heart, not of the cleanliness of the house.\nIf the heart is pure, every word and every verse shines; if the heart is stained, even temples, incense and flowers are empty.\nTherefore the merit of reciting sutras lies not in the purity of the place, but in the purity of the heart.\nIf you recite while walking, wishing protection and peace for all beings; if you recite the Buddha's name in the car, wishing the roads free of harm — all of this is a deed of blessing.\nBut if you recite with a playful heart or a lazy mind, then even seated in a golden hall, the merit is slight.\"", "\"A sutra is a mirror of the heart, not of the cleanliness of the house.\"\nA sutra is a mirror for the heart; it is not there to reflect whether a house is clean.\n\"If the heart is pure, every word and verse shines; if the heart is stained, even temples, incense and flowers are empty.\"\nIf the heart is pure, every word and every verse you recite gives off light;\nif the heart is stained by afflictions, even in a solemn Buddha hall full of incense and flowers, the merit is hollow.\n\"The merit of reciting lies not in the purity of the place, but in the purity of the heart.\"\nSo the merit of reciting sutras does not depend on whether the place is pure, but on whether the heart is pure in that moment.\n\"Reciting while walking, wishing beings peace; reciting in the car, wishing the roads free of harm — all are deeds of blessing.\"\nIf you recite while walking, blessing and protecting all beings in your heart;\nif you recite the Buddha's name in the car, praying for safe roads without accidents — these are all acts of blessing.\n\"But reciting with a playful or lazy mind, even in a golden hall, the merit is slight.\"\nBut if you recite in a joking, playful mood or with a careless, lazy mind, then even sitting in a magnificent golden hall, the merit is very slight."]}, "029": {"title": "May Eggs and Milk Be Eaten?", "summary": "On choices of food and daily goods, the author's question about purity and convenience.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: may vegetarians eat eggs and milk? Is it wrong to use leather goods?", "\"The purity of food\nlies chiefly in the heart.\"\n\"Not for the sake of taste,\nnot at the cost of a life,\none may follow conditions.\"\n\"If for convenience,\nmilk and eggs are no sin;\nif one seeks purity,\none may also keep away from them.\"\n\"In the use of things,\nlook at their causes;\nif the heart holds no cruelty,\nusing them is no fault.\"", "\"The purity of food lies chiefly in the heart.\"\nWhether food is pure depends on your intention, not only on the food itself.\n\"Not for craving of taste, not harming a life, one may follow conditions.\"\nWhat is not eaten out of greed for flavor and does not harm a life may be eaten as circumstances allow.\n\"If for convenience, milk and eggs are no sin; if one seeks purity, one may also keep away from them.\"\nIf it is for the convenience of daily life, eating eggs and milk does not create wrongdoing; if you seek a higher purity, choosing to avoid them is also good.\n\"In the use of things, look at their causes; if the heart holds no cruelty, using them is no fault.\"\nWhen using anything, look at the causes behind it — if your heart holds no intention to harm, using it is not a fault."]}, "030": {"title": "Mr. Hsu, a Fellow Practitioner, Shares How He Was Rescued by the Bodhisattva", "summary": "Mr. Hsu's own account of the turning point, trials and insights after encountering the Ksitigarbha Sutra.", "blocks": ["An account shared by Mr. Hsu, a fellow practitioner, reborn through the help of Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva\n[Testimony of Rebirth] From Exhausted Blessings to Renewed Vows: My Three Years of Being Rescued by Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva\n1. A lost past: on the edge of exhausted blessings\nThere was a time when I was deeply lost without even knowing it. In those days I did many wrong things that I am ashamed to recall today:\nNot honoring my parents: ignoring their kindness, growing arrogant.\nStealing as a child: greedy for my own desires, planting the causes of poverty and unease.\nPromiscuity: harming my reputation and the vital energy of body and mind.\nHurting others with harsh words: speaking wildly and creating countless bad connections.\nIn that state of constantly doing wrong, I could feel my \"blessings\" running out. At twenty I clearly sensed the breath of death. My body was in great pain, a suffering for which the doctors could find no cause. The dryness deep in my soul made me understand: if I did not turn back, my life would soon come to an end.\n2. Turning point and vow: Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva's rescue\nAt my most desperate, when I could hardly hold on, Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva compassionately did not give up on me. Through the Ksitigarbha Sutra, which accords with the conditions of the world, I made a sincere vow.\nThis vow changed the course of my life:\nLeading group practice: the Bodhisattva arranged for me to lead others in reciting the Ksitigarbha Sutra.\nSpeaking of causes and conditions: from a person of harsh speech, I became one who explains cause and effect to others and shares the wisdom of the Dharma.\nPracticing altruism: the Bodhisattva opened countless opportunities for me to help beings, so that in giving I could mend the cracks of my past.\n3. Adverse conditions that aid growth: two perspectives, worldly and Dharma\nAfter beginning to study Buddhism, many people assume everything will go smoothly from then on. But I must honestly tell everyone that these three years have been extremely hard. Seen by \"worldly standards\", I seem to have become very \"unlucky\":\ninjuries to my body, losing money, being blamed and scolded every day.\nBut seen through the Dharma, this is exactly what is called \"adverse conditions that aid growth\".\nThese hardships help me dissolve the heavy wrongs of my past. Without these setbacks, the gate of bodhi wisdom in my heart could not open. Every injury and every humiliation has been tempering my character, letting me realize how deep causes and conditions run.\n4. Miracles and faith: real transformation\nAlthough outer circumstances are hard, two very real miracles have happened to me, making my faith in the Dharma unshakable:\nA miracle of body and mind: from feeling at twenty that I was about to die, to now at twenty-three — since I began reciting the Ksitigarbha Sutra I have not been ill again, and the pain whose cause could never be found has vanished like smoke.\nSafety for my family: in the past disasters at home never stopped; now even when big things happen, danger turns to safety and big troubles become small.\n5. Words for beginners\nI want to tell every friend who has just encountered the Dharma: do not fear karma coming to the surface; fear only that your vow is not strong enough.\nWhen you begin to practice, life may not become perfect right away, and you may even face trials. But please remember: that is \"clearing out the rubbish\", making room in your life to hold wisdom.\nI was once lost and burdened with heavy wrongs, and still Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva pulled me up. If you too feel lost or in pain right now, please give yourself a chance and step into the world of the Ksitigarbha Sutra."]}, "031": {"title": "Hurt One Less Person When Angry, Do Not Give Up on Yourself When Low — This Is How Fate Truly Changes", "summary": "In anger, low points and success, practicing the cultivation of the heart that daily life makes possible.", "blocks": ["I asked: compassionate Bodhisattva, would you give a teaching to all beings in your own way?", "\"The suffering of beings\nis mostly not that there is no road;\nit is that the heart is in turmoil,\nso the road cannot be seen.\"\n\"People often seek:\nwhat is the right relationship,\nwhat is heaven's will,\nwhen will luck turn;\nyet few ask:\nis my heart\nclearer today than yesterday?\"\n\"Practice does not leave the world;\ncompassion does not leave daily life.\"\n\"To hurt one less person when angry,\nto not abandon oneself when low,\nto keep a humble heart when successful —\nall of this is practice.\"\n\"Do not, because of a moment's setback,\ndecide that life is hopeless;\ndo not, because of a moment's good fortune,\nforget the flowing of cause and effect.\"\n\"Some relationships\ncome to teach us to mature;\nsome suffering\ncomes to wake us up.\"\n\"To truly change one's fate\nis not to seek miraculous things;\nit is to turn one's thoughts day by day and cultivate the heart day by day.\"\n\"When you see another's suffering,\nmay you have a little more understanding;\nwhen you see your own suffering,\nmay you have a little less resentment.\"\n\"As the heart grows clearer,\nthe road ahead gradually opens.\"", "\"The suffering of beings is mostly not that there is no road; it is that the heart is in turmoil, so the road cannot be seen.\"\nMost of people's suffering is not because there is truly no way out, but because the heart is too confused to see the way.\n\"People seek the right relationship, heaven's will, when luck will turn; few ask whether their heart is clearer today than yesterday.\"\nPeople often ask: who is my true match, what is my destiny, when will good luck come? Yet very few ask: is my heart clearer today than it was yesterday?\n\"Practice does not leave the world; compassion does not leave daily life.\"\nPractice is not separate from everyday life, and compassion is not separate from how we treat people each day.\n\"To hurt one less person when angry, not abandon oneself when low, keep a humble heart when successful — all is practice.\"\nBeing able to hurt one less person when you are angry, not giving up on yourself when you are down, and staying humble when things go well — all of these are practice.\n\"Do not decide life is hopeless because of a moment's setback; do not forget cause and effect because of a moment's good fortune.\"\nDo not think life is hopeless because of a moment of disappointment; do not forget that cause and effect keep turning because of a moment of success.\n\"Some relationships come to teach us to mature; some suffering comes to wake us up.\"\nSome relationships come to help you grow up; some pain comes to wake you up.\n\"To truly change one's fate is not to seek miracles, but to turn one's thoughts and cultivate the heart day by day.\"\nTruly changing your destiny is not about praying for miracles, but about changing your thoughts and tending your heart every single day.\n\"When you see another's suffering, a little more understanding; when you see your own, a little less resentment.\"\nWhen you see others suffering, be willing to understand a little more; when you see yourself suffering, be willing to resent a little less.\n\"As the heart grows clearer, the road ahead gradually opens.\"\nAs the heart becomes clearer, the road ahead gradually opens up."]}, "032": {"title": "\"Seeking Affinity Is Not as Good as Cultivating the Heart; Seeking Gain Without Seeking Clarity Ends in Emptiness\"", "summary": "From asking about the road ahead to looking back at this heart — reminders to turn toward good and lessen attachment.", "blocks": ["A teaching from the Bodhisattva for all beings.", "Beings often ask where the road ahead lies,\nbut seldom ask where this heart is going.\n\"Many seek affinity,\nfew cultivate the heart;\nmany seek to gain,\nfew seek to understand.\"\n\"When you see others succeed, give rise to no envy;\nwhen you see others fail, give rise to no contempt.\"\n\"All things in the world\nare like spring flowers and autumn leaves —\nthey gather and they scatter.\"\n\"Today's suffering\nis not necessarily punishment;\ntoday's gain\nis not necessarily reward.\"\n\"If you wish to change your fate,\nfirst change your heart;\nif you wish to find peace,\nfirst lessen your attachments.\"\n\"Each day look at your own faults,\neach day cultivate your own heart;\none good thought arising\nsurpasses a thousand empty words.\""]}, "033": {"title": "Mr. Hsu, a Fellow Practitioner, Shares His Own Life Experience", "summary": "From the karma of speech, family and the workplace toward repentance, helping others and self-reflection.", "blocks": ["Mr. Hsu, a fellow practitioner, shares his experience again:\nSome reflections on the karma of speech, cause and effect, and the path of practice\nIn junior high school, I fell in with the wrong friends.\nIn those days I often played games in online voice chat rooms. Some of the people there came from broken families, and when we got together we loved to gossip and talk about right and wrong. When nothing was happening we felt bored, so we often stirred things up with our mouths.\nI was the same back then.\nWithout wisdom, very foolish.\nI created much karma of speech — harsh words, double-tongued talk, stirring up trouble — and besides speech, my mind was full of all kinds of mental karma; greed, anger and delusion were almost all there.\nLooking back later, I felt that many things seemed to catch up with me very quickly.\nI have a younger brother; there are two of us boys. In junior high, my father suddenly began scolding and humiliating me very easily.\nI was just preparing for senior high. I studied in a work-study program, half work and half school, and later switched to night school.\nMany things happened along the way.\nAt the time I did not understand; I only felt wronged.\nI was not doing bad things on purpose. I just wanted to start working early to share the family's financial burden. I had no plan to go to university, so I started working in high school.\nAfter graduating and starting work, I found the problems growing.\nAt work I often had poor communication with colleagues, and people reported on me behind my back. Although I met many kind helpers along the way, so many things seemed bound up with one thing — the mouth.\nSometimes it was someone else's words, sometimes a slip of my own.\nOne sentence can affect so much.\nI used to have a beautiful voice.\nIn elementary school, the teacher often asked me to read our Chinese lessons aloud, and classmates liked to listen.\nAt twenty-one, I began to encounter the Dharma.\nAt first it was simply repentance; apart from the Heart Sutra, I had not read many scriptures.\nBut life was truly not going well; it seemed that whatever I wanted to do, I could not do.\nMy father still scolded me often.\nLater I made a vow: to stop evil and cultivate good.\nThrough the coming together of conditions, I came to know Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva.\nI read the elder Venerable Master Mengcan saying that reciting the Ksitigarbha Sutra for one's parents can help them leave suffering and find happiness.\nI believed this deeply, and so truly entered the Dharma.\nI began practicing meditation, reciting mantras, reciting sutras and making vows.\nThe pain that had weighed on my heart for so long began slowly to loosen.\nThat pain had once been so heavy that I almost could not go on.\nThe real turning point was a very small thing.\nOnce I helped a man who was nearly starving. I cooked for him and gave him several meals.\nIt was strange.\nAfterwards I suddenly felt the pain in my heart let go.\nI understood:\nsometimes helping others is also saving yourself.\nWhen a single thought turns, afflictions too may begin to loosen.\nLater I also came into contact with some practice methods.\nDuring practice, there were also some unusual experiences of body and mind.\nBut I also understood:\neven if practice brings experiences, it does not mean life will have no more trials.\nBecause trials came quickly.\nOnce, after practice, I had a very distinct physical sensation in my throat.\nThe next day, while playing ball, I was suddenly struck in the throat.\nThat injury had a very great effect.\nLater it even affected my neck, breathing and head, and brought long-term insomnia.\nLater still, while doing plumbing and electrical work on a construction site, I ran into a conflict.\nIt was not that I hurt anyone with harsh words; the other man spoke improperly, and I only reasoned with him.\nUnexpectedly, he suddenly attacked me.\nBecause my throat already had an old injury, after this my head felt very heavy and painful for half a year.\nEven so, I did not take revenge, nor did I ask for compensation.\nInstead I recited sutras and dedicated the merit to the people who hurt me,\nhoping that they too could leave suffering and find happiness.\nI truly felt:\nthe Dharma can sometimes slowly turn hatred into compassion.\nIn February 2026, I began to repent more earnestly.\nI recited the holy names of Guanyin Bodhisattva and Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva, and also the Cundi mantra.\nDuring practice I also had some unusual feelings.\nFor me, I understand those experiences as the anger and attachment deep in my heart slowly rising to the surface and slowly being let go.\nAfterwards I began working while doing good and sincerely blessing others —\nwishing everyone peace and health.\nLooking back now, my physical and mental condition has indeed improved a great deal.\nI have also had some dream experiences.\nI once dreamed that Guanyin Bodhisattva bestowed sweet dew, and also dreamed that Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva appeared in an inconceivable way.\nI cannot prove these things.\nSome believe, some do not; that is fine.\nBecause this is my own experience.\nBut if I were to sum up the greatest lesson of the journey:\nthe mouth can hurt people, and it can also save people.\nThe mind can bind itself, and it can also set itself free.\nIf you once did wrong, you need not be trapped in the past forever.\nIf you are willing to turn back, willing to repent, willing to treat beings kindly — from that step on, perhaps your life has already begun to change."]}, "034": {"title": "Fate, Fortune, Feng Shui, Hidden Virtue and Study — Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva's Ultimate Answer", "summary": "With the image of a boat's frame and the current, reflecting on fate, study and the cultivation of the heart.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: people often say, \"First fate, second fortune, third feng shui, fourth accumulating hidden virtue, fifth study.\" Could the Bodhisattva share your view?", "Fate is the frame of the boat,\nfortune is the flowing water.\nFeng shui lends momentum,\nvirtue can change the course;\nstudy brings understanding of principle,\nwisdom can discern how to act.\nYet beings are often deluded,\nclinging to fate and forgetting the heart,\nseeking outside and losing the root.\nFate is not cast in iron,\nfortune is not forever fixed;\nwhen a good thought arises,\nit turns subtle causes;\nwhen good deeds accumulate long,\nthey shift the great current.\nIf you only ask about fate\nand do not cultivate yourself;\nif you only seek fortune\nand do not set your heart right,\nit is like wanting to farm a field\nwithout sowing seed.\nSo fate may be observed,\nfortune may be examined,\nfeng shui may help,\nvirtue may be deepened,\nbooks may bring clarity;\nyet gathering all these methods together,\nit still comes down to one heart."]}, "035": {"title": "On Same-Sex Love", "summary": "A question about same-sex love, unfolding through the gathering of conditions, equality and compassion.", "blocks": ["I asked the Bodhisattva for a view on same-sex love.", "\"The hearts of all beings arise from love; love is neither right nor wrong, only stained or pure.\nIf affection binds affection, one falls into samsara; if affection is transformed into compassion, one is like all Buddhas.\nMan and woman, man and man, woman and woman — all are a gathering of conditions, neither fault nor merit.\nThe body is no defect; if the heart is pure, that is the Way.\nThe practitioner, looking upon beings, should leave discrimination behind; the Buddha's eye sees all beings as equal and without difference.\""]}, "036": {"title": "Since Buddhism Teaches Non-Attachment, Is There No Need to Bow to the Buddha?", "summary": "Neither clinging to form nor slighting worship — reflecting on how reverence is lived out.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: the Diamond Sutra says that one who sees me by form or seeks me by sound walks a wrong path and cannot see the Tathagata. Does this mean there is no need to go to temples to pay respects to the Buddha and Bodhisattvas? Is bowing to the Buddha at a vihara also unnecessary? Is reverence in the heart enough?", "\"If you cling to the Buddha as being in form,\nyou are deluded by appearance;\nif you abandon form and grow irreverent,\nyou fall into emptiness.\nTo see form without clinging to form,\nto bow to the Buddha without clinging to the Buddha —\nthis is right practice.\nIf you take form for the Buddha,\nyou lose the Buddha;\nif you use emptiness to destroy form,\nyou also lose the Buddha.\nThe Buddha is not in the hall,\nnor apart from the hall;\nthe heart is the master,\nconduct is the proof.\"", "So the Bodhisattva means: the Buddha image is not the Buddha, but my attitude toward the image reflects my heart?\nIt is like loving someone: although love in the heart is enough, sometimes we still need to hug them, to write them a letter. In those five or ten minutes of bowing to the Buddha, we learn to focus and to let go. Is this understanding correct?", "\"The Buddha image is not the Buddha,\nyet your heart toward the image\nis the appearing of your heart.\nIf you slight the image,\nit is not the image you slight,\nbut your own heart;\nif you cling to the image,\nit is not love for the Buddha,\nbut bondage to form.\nIn the time of bowing,\nwhat matters is not long or short,\nbut whether you can,\nwithin a single breath,\nlet go of discrimination.\nTo keep the mind unscattered for five minutes\nsurpasses a whole day with the mind absent.\""]}, "037": {"title": "Three Teachings the Bodhisattva Gave Me: Reflections on a Ten-Year Relationship", "summary": "Three personal questions on love with the author's notes: meeting, owing, and after the affinity ends.", "blocks": ["Only now can this humble student begin to face my past relationship honestly. I am grateful for the Bodhisattva's teachings all along, which gave me the courage to share them widely online. I also hope that all of you who read of my experience may come to sense the compassion of Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva and the truth of impermanence.\n※ These are teachings for my own personal conditions. Conditions are countless; do not take one example as true for all under heaven. Please weigh them carefully as a reference.\n[The Bodhisattva's teaching on publishing this text]", "\"Good man,\nyour wish to circulate what you have recorded\nis a praiseworthy intention;\nyet know that once words go out,\na thousand people will understand them a thousand ways.\"\n\"If you wish to benefit beings,\nlet them grow in wisdom,\nnot in attachment.\"\n\"If you wish to speak of causes and conditions,\nlet beings know cause and effect,\nnot fatalism.\"\n\"What you have recorded\nloses nothing of the main meaning;\nthe feelings you describe\nare sincere and worthy.\"\n\"Yet remember:\nthis was spoken because of one person's suffering,\nnot as a law fixed for all people.\"\n\"Conditions are countless;\ndo not take one example as true for all under heaven.\"\n\"If you add one sentence\nso later readers know that their conditions differ,\nit will be more complete.\"\n\"If you add one sentence\nso beings know that cultivating the heart matters more than seeking answers,\nit will accord better with the Dharma.\"\n\"When you publish,\nseek no praise,\nseek no approval,\nseek no proof.\"\n\"May those who read it have a little less resentment\nand a little more clarity;\na little less hatred,\na little more kindness.\"\n\"Circulated in this way,\nit will bring benefit.\"\n\"With such an intention,\nthe effort will not be in vain.\"", "(End of the Bodhisattva's guidance on publishing.)\n════════════════════════════════════════════\n==== Below are the teachings on my previous relationship ====", "First question: why, after ten years together, did it come to nothing?", "\"Conditions arise not for the sake of an outcome,\nand an outcome is not the original intent of the conditions.\nYour meeting with them\nwas not the final station, but a point on the way.\nTen years were not wasted;\nthey let each heart know what it truly wished for.\nHad you never met,\nyou would not know what you value;\nhad you not walked long together,\nthey would not know what they could not accept.\nTo walk together for a stretch in love\nis already one kind of completeness;\nit need not last to the end\nto be called faithful.\"", "Second question: who owed whom?", "\"Not owing,\nnor repaying.\nWhen conditions come,\nthey are not an ending,\nbut a revealing of the heart.\nIf you judge causes by suffering,\nall conditions are mistakes;\nif you judge causes by awakening,\nall conditions are kindnesses.\nYou and they\ndid not come to wound each other,\nbut to reflect each other.\nOne saw clearly 'what I want',\nthe other saw clearly 'what I cannot do';\nneither is a fault,\nneither is a sin.\nThe wound came from not fitting together,\nnot from meeting.\nWithout this affinity,\nyou would still not know the weight of your own heart;\nwithout this parting,\nthey would still not see the boundary they keep.\"", "Third question: would it have been better to end it earlier?", "\"Neither early nor late;\nit ends when the conditions ripen.\nHad you ended it then,\nwith the heart not yet clear,\nending would have been fleeing;\nwere you to stay now,\nwith love already spent,\nstaying would become harm.\nThat you can stop now\nis not late awakening,\nbut the arrival of the right conditions.\nTo insist on calling 'early' good\nis to blame the past heart with hindsight —\nneither wise\nnor kind.\nTheir pain now\nis not because you ended it late,\nbut because the feelings were not yet spent;\nyour pain now\nis not because of when it ended,\nbut because your heart is still kind.\"", "(The three questions are finished. My own reflections follow.)\n——————————————————————————————————————\nPersonal note\nWe should know that what is called \"letting go\" —\nis not asking you to forget the past;\nis not asking you to deny the past;\nis not asking you to force yourself to cut off all feeling.\nRather — it is no longer letting past events bind your present heart.\nThis teaching does not tell everyone that \"relationships need not be cherished\", and still less does it encourage giving up when things are hard. Quite the opposite: precisely because I once gave sincerely, I can look back with a clear conscience even though the affinity has ended. If the affinity remains, cherish and tend it; if the affinity has ended, learn to let go. Perhaps this is the lesson that causes and conditions have taught me.\nI make these teachings public not to complain, nor to blame anyone, but because I know that many people in the world, like me, blame themselves after a parting and keep chewing over \"if only\" in their memories. Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva's teaching did not give me an \"answer\", but it gave me a \"direction\" — no longer asking who was right or wrong, but asking myself: what did this meeting teach me?\nThat learning is the greatest blessing of my life."]}, "038": {"title": "If Cause, Effect and Karma Are Already Fixed, What Can a Good Thought and Diligent Practice Still Change?", "summary": "Old causes are already sown, yet today we can still add new ones — asking how practice changes direction.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: if cause, effect and karmic force are already fixed, then what, in the end, can a being's single good thought and diligent practice in this moment still change? Please, compassionate Bodhisattva, give a teaching.", "\"Good man,\nyou ask:\n'If cause, effect and karmic force are already fixed,\nwhat can a being's present thought of good\nstill change?'\"\n\"Know this:\nif karma were wholly fixed,\nthe Buddhas would not need to teach the Dharma;\nthe Bodhisattvas would not need to deliver beings;\nand there would be no meaning in beings' practice.\"\n\"Cause and effect is not fatalism;\ncause and effect is causes and conditions.\"\n\"Past causes\ncan become today's effects;\ntoday's causes\nwill in turn become the effects of the future.\"\n\"Beings think\neffects cannot be changed;\nyet they do not know\nthat effects already ripened\nmay be hard to turn completely,\nwhile effects not yet ripened\ncan be turned by present causes.\"\n\"It is like a seed in the soil\nnot yet sprouted;\nwater it diligently with sweet dew,\nand the good shoot will grow;\nlet the weeds run wild,\nand the good shoot will be smothered.\"\n\"A single thought of compassion\ndoes not erase old causes,\nbut it can grow new ones.\"\n\"A single thought of repentance\ndoes not change yesterday,\nbut it can change tomorrow.\"\n\"So the practitioner\ndoes not struggle against cause and effect,\nbut parts from ignorance.\"\n\"To know karma without fearing karma,\nto trust causes and diligently plant causes —\nthis is called right view.\"\n\"Your single thought toward good today\nis the beginning of tomorrow's light.\"\n(End of teaching.)"]}, "039": {"title": "Asking Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva How the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas Attain Perfect, Complete Awakening", "summary": "Through shame, cause and effect, beings and one's own heart — a question on the path to awakening.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: by what method did you practice to attain perfect, complete awakening?", "You ask:\nby what Dharma did I once attain perfect, complete awakening?\"\n\"Know this:\nthough the Buddhas appear in the world earlier and later,\nand the Bodhisattvas differ in vows and practice,\nthe truth they realize\nis not two.\"\n\"It is not a path of mine alone,\nnor does each Buddha have a separate path.\"\n\"All, by awakening to the nature of mind,\nperfecting compassion,\nand benefiting beings,\nreach the other shore.\"\n\"First take conscience and a sense of shame as teacher,\nnext take cause and effect as teacher,\nthen take beings as teacher,\nand finally take your own heart as teacher.\"\n\"Seeing suffering, make vows;\nseeing delusion, feel compassion;\nseeing your nature, break attachment;\nseeing emptiness, do not dwell in emptiness.\"\n\"It is not achieved in one sitting,\nnor finished in one awakening.\"\n\"Practicing through eons as many as dust motes,\nevery act is the Way;\nwith awareness in every thought,\nevery place is a gate.\"\n\"If you ask for a shortcut to Buddhahood,\nknow this:\nthere is no shortcut.\"\n\"If you ask for the nearest road to Buddhahood,\nknow this:\narousing bodhicitta\nis the nearest road.\"\n(End of teaching.)"]}, "040": {"title": "\"Without Living Through Another's Pain, Do Not Urge Them to Be Good; Had You Lived Through Mine, You Might Not Be as Good as Me\" — Is This Against Buddhist Thought?", "summary": "Facing another's suffering, reflecting on the measure of understanding, persuasion and compassion.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: how does the Bodhisattva see this saying — \"Without living through another's pain, do not urge them to be good; had you lived through my pain, you might not be as good as me\"? Is it against Buddhist thought?", "\"This saying is not wholly wrong, yet it stops at ordinary feeling.\nTo see suffering and hold back from urging is the wisdom of self-protection;\nto know suffering and give rise to compassion is the practice of a Bodhisattva.\nIf you make your own suffering a gate that shuts others out,\nthen suffering becomes self-attachment, not a cause of liberation.\"", "This saying is not completely wrong, but it stays at the level of ordinary people (based on self-protection and emotional reaction).\nSeeing others suffer and choosing not to persuade or interfere is a wisdom of self-protection (a worldly wisdom).\nKnowing the true nature of suffering and therefore giving rise to compassion, willing to help beings leave suffering, is the practice of a Bodhisattva.\nIf you use your own suffering as an excuse to shut others out and refuse to grow, that suffering becomes a solid \"self-attachment\" instead of a condition for liberation."]}, "041": {"title": "Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva's Teaching: Non-Self and the Causes of Goodness and Wisdom", "summary": "From the relation between the \"self\" and good deeds, an inquiry into non-self, compassion and wisdom.", "blocks": ["I asked the Bodhisattva: if there is no \"self\", where do goodness and wisdom come from? Is the \"self\" we cling to really necessary?", "\"Non-self is not nothing;\nself is not real.\nBeings cling to self,\nand so give rise to discrimination;\nbodhi is free of self,\nand so gives rise to compassion.\nGoodness is not made by the self;\nit is formed by causes and conditions.\nWisdom is not owned by the self;\nthe original nature is clear of itself.\nIf you cling to there being a self,\ngoodness easily becomes pride;\nif you cling to there being no self,\nyou fall into empty stillness.\nLeave the views of both extremes,\nand the Middle Way appears.\nWhat you ask now\nis close to the heart of the Dharma.\nKnow that practice\nis not destroying the body,\nbut breaking the attachment to self.\nWhen attachment is gone, there is clarity;\nwithin clarity there is compassion;\nwithin compassion there is wisdom;\nwithin wisdom there is no self.\"", "\"'Non-self' does not mean there is nothing at all; 'self' does not mean there truly is a solid, unchanging me. Because beings cling to the idea of a 'me', they develop a discriminating mind; and bodhi-wisdom, precisely by leaving behind attachment to 'me', can give rise to true compassion.\nGood deeds are not created by 'me'; they come about through the coming together of causes and conditions. Wisdom is not something 'I' own; it is the original nature naturally revealing itself.\nIf you cling to 'there really is a me', good deeds easily turn into pride (feeling that you are doing good and are above others); but if, the other way round, you cling to 'there is no me at all', you fall to the side of empty stillness, passivity and inaction.\nOnly when you leave these two extreme views does the Middle Way appear.\nThe question you ask now is already very close to the core of the Dharma.\nKnow that practice is not about destroying this body (or destroying yourself as a person), but about breaking the attachment to 'me'.\nOnce attachment is removed, wisdom appears; within wisdom there is naturally compassion; within compassion wisdom is naturally contained; and in such wisdom there is no 'me' in charge.\""]}, "042": {"title": "Seeking First, Then Letting Go — Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva on the Middle Way of Doing Good", "summary": "Doing good may begin with wanting something, then slowly let go of clinging to reward.", "blocks": ["I asked the Bodhisattva: people often say that doing good in hope of reward is impure, yet someone who feels nothing about good and evil and does nothing at all seems even worse. With what kind of heart should we do good for it to be right? Where is the line between goodness that seeks merit and goodness from a true heart?", "When first practicing goodness,\nmost have something they seek;\nthis is no great fault,\nand better than not acting.\nDoing good because of seeking,\nthe seed of goodness is already planted;\npracticing long without turning back,\nthe heart gradually purifies itself.\nIf you seek fame and gain,\ngoodness easily runs out;\nif you seek liberation,\ngoodness gradually becomes selfless.\nDo not worry that your first intention\nis not yet pure;\nworry only that after long practice\nyou are unwilling to turn your heart.\nMerit is not a sin;\nclinging to merit becomes an obstacle.\nTo know that goodness can be cultivated,\nto know that merit can be let go —\nthis is the Middle Way.\nIt is like a child\nfirst learning to walk;\nthough encouraged by rewards,\nin the end it stands on its own.\nGoodness is the same:\nfrom having something to gain,\nit gradually comes to seeking nothing.\nSeeking nothing is not cutting off,\nbut the fulfillment of compassion;\nwhen the heart forgets the self,\ngood deeds flow on constantly.", "Most people who are just beginning to do good carry a wish to get something. This is no great fault — it is still better than not doing good at all. Doing good because you want something still plants the seed of goodness; as long as you keep at it for a long time without turning back, your heart will naturally become purer.\nIf what you seek is fame and gain, such goodness easily runs out one day; if what you seek is liberation, such goodness gradually becomes selfless.\nDo not worry that your heart was not pure enough at the start; what you should really worry about is still being unwilling to change your mind after practicing for a long time.\nMerit itself is not a fault; clinging to merit is what becomes an obstacle. Knowing that goodness must be cultivated, and also knowing that merit can be let go — this is the Middle Way.\nIt is just like a small child learning to walk: they may only take steps because of an adult's rewards and encouragement, but in the end they still learn to stand and walk on their own.\nDoing good follows the same principle: from first seeking and gaining something, you slowly come to a state of seeking nothing.\nAnd this \"seeking nothing\" is not giving up good deeds, but the natural fulfillment of compassion; when the heart has forgotten even the \"me\", good deeds keep flowing on naturally, like water."]}, "043": {"title": "What Is Emptiness?", "summary": "Through flowers blooming and falling and clouds gathering into rain, reading the images of dependent arising and emptiness.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: what is emptiness? I humbly ask the Bodhisattva to teach everyone.", "When beings hear of emptiness, most grow afraid,\nthinking emptiness means that nothing at all exists.\nSo they lose its meaning and fall into the view of annihilation.\nEmptiness is not non-existence; it is empty of self-nature.\nThings exist dependent on conditions; when conditions scatter, they cease.\nA flower blooming is not permanent; a flower falling is not annihilation.\nClouds gather into rain; when the rain is spent, it returns to emptiness.\nCling to existence, and you give rise to craving;\ncling to non-existence, and you give rise to despair.\nTo leave both existence and non-existence is called the Middle Way.\nKnowing emptiness without forsaking beings, compassion grows ever wider;\nawakening to emptiness without leaving the world, cause and effect remain clear.\nIf you see the meaning of emptiness, you do not destroy the ways of the world;\nif you dwell in the appearance of emptiness, you lose true emptiness instead.\nTherefore the practitioner should contemplate all dharmas:\ndependently arisen, empty by nature; empty by nature, dependently arisen —\nthe two are without difference.", "When beings hear the word \"emptiness\", most become afraid;\nthey think \"emptiness\" means everything is gone, cut off into nothing.\nSo they lose the true meaning of emptiness and fall into the wrong view of annihilation.\n\"Emptiness\" does not mean \"nothing exists\";\nit means that nothing has an independent, unchanging self-essence.\nPhenomena exist by depending on causes and conditions;\nwhen the conditions disperse, the phenomena disperse too.\nA flower blooms, but it does not stay forever (it is not permanent);\na flower falls, but it is not completely wiped out into nothingness (it will bloom again next year).\nClouds gather and form rain; after the rain falls, it returns to emptiness.\nBecause you cling to \"existence\", you develop craving;\nbecause you cling to \"non-existence\", you develop despair.\nLeaving both extremes — clinging to existence and clinging to non-existence — is called the Middle Way.\nKnowing emptiness does not mean giving up compassion; rather, compassion becomes even wider;\nrealizing emptiness does not mean leaving the world; cause and effect remain perfectly clear.\nIf you truly see the meaning of emptiness, you will not undermine the laws by which the world works;\nif you cling to an \"image of emptiness\" (thinking emptiness is a kind of state or realm), you lose true emptiness instead.\nSo practitioners should observe that all phenomena\nare \"dependently arisen and empty by nature\";\nand because they are \"empty by nature\", they can \"arise dependently\".\nThese two are not two different things, but two sides of one whole."]}, "044": {"title": "After a Loved One Passes, Is Missing Them a Form of Attachment?", "summary": "There is no need to force ourselves to forget those we love; let longing walk alongside going on with life.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: may I trouble the Bodhisattva to teach all beings — \"After a loved one passes away, is missing them a kind of attachment?\"", "When a loved one departs, what sin is there in longing?\nRemembering their kindness, the heart is moved;\nrecalling their voice and face, tears fall of themselves.\nThis is not attachment; it is human feeling.\nIf longing does not obstruct, if remembering still lets you act,\nthe deeper the longing, the more the good heart grows.\nIf you cling to their form and will not let them go,\nif you resent impermanence and suffer day after day,\nthis is clinging to appearances.\nSo know this: longing may remain; attachment may be released.\nTo love a person, you need not imprison your own heart.\nTo remember their kindness, you may continue their goodness.\nIf you turn longing into cultivating blessings and dedicating merit,\nthen within sorrow there is a vow, within pain there is compassion.\nThough a person has left the world, the bond is not wholly broken;\nthe form departs but the kindness remains, the appearance departs but the goodness endures.\nDo not force yourself to forget the one you love;\nnor hold your grief and never let it go.\nLet the longing come, let the tears flow;\nwhen they have flowed, still walk on.\nThis is letting go — not forgetting.", "When a loved one has passed away, is missing them some kind of wrong?\nRemembering their kindness, the heart is naturally moved;\nrecalling their voice and face, tears naturally fall.\nThis is not attachment; it is real human feeling between people.\nIf missing them does not stop you from living normally and still lets you keep going,\nthen the deeper you miss them, the more your good heart will grow.\nIf you cling to their past form and will not allow them to truly leave;\nif you resent impermanence and trap yourself in pain every day —\nthat is what \"clinging to appearances\" is.\nSo know this: you may miss them; you can let go of attachment.\nTo truly love someone, you do not have to lock your heart in the past.\nRemembering their kindness, you can carry on the goodness they left behind.\nIf you can turn this longing into the strength to cultivate blessings and dedicate merit,\nthen there will be a vow within the sorrow, and compassion within the pain.\nAlthough the person has left, the bond is not completely broken;\nthe body is gone, but the kindness remains; the appearance has vanished, but the good deeds remain.\nDo not force yourself to forget the one you love;\nbut do not cling tightly to grief either.\nAllow the longing to come, allow the tears to flow;\nonce they have flowed, keep walking forward.\nThis is letting go, not forgetting."]}, "045": {"title": "Is the Bodhisattva's Wish to Save Beings a Kind of Desire? A Soul-Searching Question From Kevin Tsai", "summary": "A vow has direction, desire has grasping — reflecting on the difference between a great vow and craving.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva:\nIn an interview, Kevin Tsai (Cai Kangyong) once said publicly that he wanted to ask the Bodhisattva: isn't wanting to deliver all beings itself a desire?\nIf you have a desire, won't you feel frustrated at the moments it is not fulfilled?", "There are two kinds of wanting: one is grasping, one is a great vow.\nThey share the name of desire, but the heart is different.\nIf you want fame and gain, seeking them for yourself,\nyou resent it when you do not get it, and fear losing it once you have it — this desire binds.\nIf you vow that beings may leave suffering and find happiness,\nthough you wish it accomplished, you do not force it by your own strength;\nthough you seek and do not attain, you do not retreat from goodness.\nTherefore a great vow is not the same as craving.\nA vow has direction; desire has grasping.\nA vow can let go of the result; desire must seize the result.\nWhen a Bodhisattva makes a vow, it is not that they want no result,\nbut that they are not bound by the result.\nIf you try to deliver beings and they are not delivered, you still do not lose compassion;\nif you do good and no one knows, you still wish to do good;\nif you seek the Dharma and have not yet realized it, you still do not turn back.\nThis is a vow.\nIf your heart wishes to deliver beings only to prove yourself,\nonly to accomplish yourself, only to receive the praise of beings,\nthen though your mouth speaks of compassion, your heart is still grasping.\nSo do not ask whether there is desire or not; ask this heart:\nIs it for myself, or for others? Clinging to results, or practicing the vow?\nIf, when a vow is not fulfilled, you can rest at peace;\nif, when seeking is not fulfilled, you give rise to anger —\nthe difference between them can be seen right here.\nWhen beings do good, they may at first have something they seek;\nwhen Bodhisattvas make vows, they too are not without seeking.\nOnly, as practice goes deeper, they gradually leave the self and gradually leave gaining.\nIf a thought of compassion still holds a seeking heart, do not rush to blame yourself;\nsimply see it clearly, and gradually let it go.\nTrue non-seeking is not wishing for nothing;\nit is vowing without being bound, acting without clinging.", "There are two kinds of desire: one is \"grasping\", and one is a \"great vow\".\nAlthough both are called \"desire\", the nature of the heart behind them is different.\nIf you pursue fame and gain, seeking them for yourself,\nyou complain when you do not get them and fear losing them when you do — this kind of desire binds you.\nIf you vow to help beings leave suffering and find happiness,\nthough you hope it will come true, you do not use your own force to demand the result;\neven if it does not happen, you do not lose your good heart because of it.\nSo a great vow is not the same as greed.\nA vow has a direction; a desire has grasping.\nA vow can let go of the result; a desire must hold on to the result.\nWhen Bodhisattvas make vows, it is not that they do not want results,\nbut that they are not tied up by results.\nIf you try to help beings across and do not succeed, you still do not lose compassion;\nif you do good and no one knows, you still want to keep doing good;\nif you seek the Dharma and have not yet realized it, you still do not turn back.\nThis is what a vow is.\nIf you want to help beings only to prove yourself,\nonly to achieve something for yourself, only to win people's praise,\nthen even if your mouth speaks of compassion, your heart is still grasping.\nSo do not ask \"do I have desire or not?\"\nAsk your own heart: Is it for me, or for others? Am I clinging to the result, or living out the vow?\nIf you can stay at peace when a vow is not fulfilled,\nbut become angry when what you sought is not obtained —\nthe difference between the two can be seen right there.\nWhen people do good, it is fine to want something at first;\nwhen Bodhisattvas make vows, they are not completely without wishes either.\nIt is only that, as practice deepens, one gradually moves away from \"me\" and away from \"getting\".\nIf a compassionate thought still carries a wish to get something, do not rush to blame yourself;\njust see it, and gradually let it go.\nTrue \"seeking nothing\" is not wanting nothing at all;\nit is \"vowing without being bound, acting without clinging\" — having a vow but not being tied by it; taking action but not clinging to the result."]}, "046": {"title": "Telling the Truth Is Not Always Good; a Lie Is Not Always Evil", "summary": "When truth and kindness seem to conflict, reflecting on speech, timing and a heart that does not deceive.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: \"Bodhisattva, does a white lie told so as not to hurt someone break the precept against false speech? If truth and compassion conflict, which should come first?\"", "The precept against false speech does not only examine true or false; it also observes the heart.\nIf you knowingly say what is false with a deceiving heart, causing others harm, this is false speech.\nIf it is to protect someone, so they do not suffer, with no heart to deceive or harm, and the words are a skillful means — here you must discern carefully.\nTrue words can wound, false words can also harm; between compassion and truth, do not lightly choose one and discard the other.\nIf true words are spoken only to vent, though the words are true, it is not compassion.\nIf kind words are not to deceive, not for your own gain, but only wishing to stop suffering, then consider skillful means.\nIf you can speak the truth without wounding, speak the truth; if you can say it later, say it later; if you can say less, say less; you need not state every truth outright.\nIf you cannot speak in the moment, you may stay silent; if silence cannot protect, use skillful words.\nBut you must not use the name of compassion to cover your own greed, fear and selfishness.\nSo keeping the precept against false speech is not merely guarding the letter of words; it is guarding a true heart, a kind heart, a heart that does not deceive.\nTruth without compassion easily becomes a sharp blade; compassion without wisdom can also become foolishness.\nTherefore, with wisdom, let truth and compassion fulfill each other.\nWhere you can be true, be true; where you can be kind, be kind; when skillful means are unavoidable, let the heart not deceive others, and afterwards not deceive yourself.\nPracticing like this, you come close to skillfulness.", "Keeping the precept against false speech is not only checking whether what you say is true or false; you must also look at the state of the heart behind the words.\nIf you know something is false and say it with the intent to deceive, so that others are harmed, that is false speech.\nIf it is to protect someone, not wanting them to suffer, with no intent to deceive or harm in your heart, and you use a skillful way of speaking — this needs careful discernment.\nTrue words can hurt people; false words can also harm people. Between compassion and truth, you cannot simply say \"just pick this one\".\nIf you speak the truth only to vent your emotions, even if the content is true, it is not compassion.\nIf you speak kindly not to deceive, not for your own benefit, but only hoping to lessen the other person's pain, then you need to consider what the skillful way is.\nIf you can tell the truth without hurting them, tell the truth;\nif you can say it later, say it later;\nif you can say less, say less;\nthere is no need to state every truth outright.\nIf you truly cannot speak in the moment, you can stay silent;\nif silence cannot protect the other person, then use skillful words.\nBut you must not use the name of \"compassion\" to cover up your own greed, fear or selfishness.\nSo keeping the precept against false speech does not ask people to cling rigidly to the literal truth of words;\nit asks you to guard three kinds of heart: a truthful heart, a kind heart, and a heart that does not deceive.\nTruthful words without compassion easily become a knife that hurts;\ncompassionate actions without wisdom can also become foolish.\nSo you should use wisdom to let truth and compassion bring each other to completion.\nWhere you can be truthful, be truthful; where you can be kind, be kind;\nwhen you must use a skillful means, do not deceive others in your heart, and do not deceive yourself afterwards.\nPracticing in this way comes close to the wisdom of skillful means."]}, "047": {"title": "I Keep Doing Good — Why Does Suffering Still Find Me?", "summary": "When suffering comes, do not rush to doubt your good deeds or blame yourself; first tend to the suffering in front of you.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: Bodhisattva, I believe in cause and effect and do good to accumulate virtue, yet hardships keep coming one after another. At such times, should I doubt that I have not practiced enough, or doubt cause and effect itself?", "You do good and meet suffering; do not rush to doubt goodness, and do not rush to blame yourself.\nThe principle of cause and effect is not that if you plant good today, there will be no suffering tomorrow.\nThe forming of a single result is the coming together of many conditions; the arrival of a single suffering is not born of one cause.\nPast causes, present conditions, the actions of others and the changes of the world may all meet at this moment.\nSo doing good is not a bargain with heaven and earth.\nDo not say: 'I have done good — why must I still suffer?'\nIf you trade goodness for smooth fortune, and smooth fortune does not come, the good heart retreats; such goodness still seeks something.\nYet neither should you, whenever suffering comes, blame yourself, saying: 'It must be that I have not practiced enough; my karmic obstacles must be deep.'\nThis too is attachment.\nSome suffering is not because you are not good;\nsome hardship is not sent to punish you.\nBirth, aging, sickness and death, meeting and parting, gain and loss — the world is impermanent, and all beings meet these things.\nThe benefit of practice is not a guarantee of a life without hardship,\nbut that when suffering comes, you have a little less resentment than before and a little more clarity;\nyou create a little less evil and give rise to a little more compassion.\nIf you do good only to avoid suffering, when suffering comes your faith will waver.\nIf you know that good is simply what should be done, then even when adversity appears you still do not repay evil with evil;\nonly then do you know that your good roots are growing.\nAs for cause and effect, do not understand it as an account book that rewards good and punishes evil.\nGood causes have good results, evil causes have evil results;\nyet results ripen slowly or quickly, in plain sight or unseen, as conditions interweave — more than an ordinary person can see all at once.\nIf you are suffering, first deal with the suffering before you;\nif it can be treated, treat it; if it can be changed, change it; if it can be avoided, avoid it; if you can seek help, seek help.\nDo not, because you speak of cause and effect, leave suffering untreated;\nnor, because you meet suffering, conclude that doing good is useless.\nWhen suffering comes, there is no need to doubt cause and effect first, nor to doubt yourself first.\nFirst ask: 'In this situation, what can I do right now?'\nWhere you can change, have the courage to change;\nwhere you cannot change, learn to bear it;\nwhere you should seek help, do not let practice take the place of seeking help.\nWhat you should truly observe is not:\n'How much good have I done, and how much blessing has it bought?'\nbut:\n'After these hardships, is my heart clearer and more compassionate than before?'\nIf so, though the suffering has not ended, you are already walking the Way.", "You do good yet meet hardship; do not rush to doubt your good deeds, and do not rush to blame yourself.\nThe principle of cause and effect is not that if you plant a good cause today, there will be no pain tomorrow.\nA single result takes many conditions coming together; a single hardship is not caused by one reason alone either.\nPast causes, present conditions, other people's actions and changes in the world may all come together at the same moment.\nSo doing good is not making a deal with heaven and earth.\nDo not say: \"I have already done good — why am I still made to suffer?\"\nIf you do good only to buy good fortune, then when good fortune does not come, your good heart fades; such goodness still wants something in return.\nBut neither should you blame yourself whenever hardship comes, saying: \"It must be that I haven't practiced well enough; my karmic obstacles must be too heavy.\"\nThat too is a kind of attachment.\nSome suffering is not because you are bad;\nsome difficulties are not there to punish you.\nBirth, aging, sickness and death, meeting and parting, gaining and losing — the world is impermanent by nature, and every being meets these.\nThe benefit of practice is not a guarantee that your life will have no hardship;\nit is that when hardship comes, you have a little less resentment than before and a little more understanding;\nyou create a little less harm and give rise to a little more compassion.\nIf you do good only to avoid suffering, your faith will be shaken when suffering arrives.\nIf you know that doing good is simply right, and even when adversity appears you still do not repay harm with harm,\nthen you truly know that your good roots are growing.\nAs for cause and effect, do not think of it as an account book of rewards and punishments.\nGood causes bring good results, and harmful causes bring harmful results;\nbut results ripen fast or slow, obviously or subtly, and conditions are so intertwined that ordinary people cannot see it all at once.\nIf you are facing hardship, deal with the problem in front of you first;\nif you can see a doctor, see a doctor; if you can change something, change it; if you can avoid it, avoid it; if you can ask for help, ask for help.\nDo not let the idea of cause and effect make you leave suffering untreated;\nand do not decide that doing good is useless just because you meet hardship.\nWhen hardship comes, there is no need to doubt cause and effect first, nor to doubt yourself first.\nFirst ask: \"At this moment, what can I do now?\"\nWhere things can change, have the courage to change them;\nwhere they cannot, learn to accept them;\nwhere you need help, do not use \"practice\" as a substitute for asking for help.\nWhat you should truly look at is not:\n\"How much good have I done, and how much blessing has it earned me?\"\nbut:\n\"After going through these hardships, is my heart clearer and more compassionate than before?\"\nIf so, then although the hardship is not over, you are already walking the path of practice."]}, "048": {"title": "After Seeing the Darkest Side of Human Nature, Should We Still Trust People?", "summary": "Compassion can be boundless, but trust must have measure — keeping wisdom and boundaries for kindness.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva: after seeing so much selfishness, betrayal, violence and deception, should one still believe in human nature? How can one be neither naive, nor lose compassion and faith in beings because of it?", "You see the evil in the human world and ask whether you can still trust people.\nThis question does not arise because you lack compassion, but because the suffering you have seen grows deeper.\nBeings have good and also evil;\nthey have compassion and also greed, anger and delusion.\nSo the practitioner need not believe that everyone is good in order to be compassionate.\nTo know that someone may do evil, and still not wish to treat them with evil — this is compassion.\nIf you clearly know they deceive and still let them deceive, this is not compassion; it is lack of wisdom.\nIf you clearly know they harm and still do not avoid the harm, this is not kindness; it is a failure to protect.\nThe Dharma does not teach you to take evil people for good people;\nnor does it teach you, because you see evil people, to decide that all beings are evil.\nWhat you should believe is not that 'everyone can be trusted';\nwhat you should believe is that the hearts of beings are not forever unchanging.\nThe greedy today may come to know contentment;\nthe angry today may come to repent;\nthe deluded today may also awaken.\nYet while they have not yet changed, you must still guard against their evil.\nCompassion can be boundless; trust must have measure.\nCompassion is wishing them free of suffering; trust must be proven by their conduct.\nSo you may be compassionate toward someone, and still not lend them money;\nyou may wish someone to turn toward good, and no longer work with them;\nyou may forgive someone, and not let them hurt you again;\nyou may hold no hatred, and still keep your distance.\nNone of this goes against compassion.\nIf, because you were once deceived, you never trust anyone again,\nthen one person's evil makes countless people who never harmed you suffer your suspicion first.\nIf, wanting to believe in human nature, you ignore every evil deed,\nthen compassion becomes delusion.\nSo learn:\nsee good without blind belief, see evil without despair.\nIn judging a person, observe their long-term conduct; when you hear their words, see whether they are put into practice.\nGive trust gradually; set boundaries first.\nA single deception may be forgiven; whether to trust again depends on whether they have truly changed.\nThat beings are worthy of compassion does not mean all beings are worthy of trust.\nWhat you must truly guard is not a fantasy that 'human nature must be good';\nit is that, having seen all the darkness of the world, you do not let the darkness enter your own heart.\nSeeing others selfish, you still learn to give;\nseeing others betray, you still keep faith;\nseeing others violent, you do not meet violence with violence;\nseeing others deceive, you still keep to the truth.\nIn this way, the evil of the world has not turned you into the kind of person you detest.\nThis is rare indeed.\nNot naive — that is wisdom; not despairing — that is compassion.\nKnow evil and guard against evil, see suffering and pity suffering; do not indulge, nor hate.\nTo walk like this is the Middle Way.", "You see all kinds of evil in the human world, and then ask: \"Should I still believe in human nature?\"\nThis question arises not because you lack compassion, but because you have seen more and more suffering.\nAmong beings there is goodness, and there is also evil;\nthere is compassion, and there is also greed, anger and delusion.\nSo practitioners do not need to treat every person as good in order to be compassionate toward people.\nKnowing someone might do wrong, yet still not wanting to repay wrong with wrong — that is what compassion is.\nIf you know someone is deceiving you and let them go on deceiving you, that is not compassion; that is lacking wisdom.\nIf you know someone will bring harm and do not protect yourself, that is not kindness; that is a lack of protection.\nThe Dharma does not teach you to \"treat bad people as good people\";\nnor does it teach you to \"decide all beings are evil because you have seen bad people\".\nWhat is truly worth believing is not that \"everyone is trustworthy\";\nwhat is truly worth believing is that \"people's hearts are not forever unchanging\".\nThose who are greedy today may later learn contentment;\nthose who are angry today may later repent;\nthose who are confused today may also later awaken.\nBut until they have changed, you still need to guard against their wrongdoing.\nCompassion can be unlimited, but trust must have limits.\nCompassion is \"hoping they are free of suffering\"; trust must be proven by their actions.\nSo you can be compassionate toward someone and still not lend them money;\nyou can hope someone turns toward good and still no longer work with them;\nyou can forgive someone and still not let them hurt you again;\nyou can hold no resentment and still keep your distance.\nNone of these go against compassion.\nIf, because you were once deceived, you stop trusting anyone at all,\nthen one person's wrong ends up making countless people who never hurt you bear your suspicion first.\nIf, for the sake of \"believing in human nature\", you ignore every warning sign of wrongdoing,\nthen your compassion turns into foolishness.\nSo learn to\nsee goodness without believing blindly,\nand see evil without falling into despair.\nTo judge a person, look at their behavior over a long time;\nwhen you hear their words, see whether they actually follow through.\nGive trust slowly, and set boundaries first.\nOne deception can be forgiven;\nbut whether to trust again depends on whether they have truly changed.\nThat beings deserve your compassion does not mean every being deserves your trust.\nWhat you should truly hold on to is not the fantasy that \"human nature must be good\";\nit is not letting the darkness move into your own heart because you have seen so much darkness in the world.\nWhen you see others being selfish, keep learning to give;\nwhen you see others betray, keep your word;\nwhen you see others being violent, do not answer violence with violence;\nwhen you see others deceive, keep holding to the truth.\nIn this way, the evil of the world has not turned you into the kind of person you dislike.\nThat is what is rare.\nNot being naive comes from wisdom;\nnot despairing comes from compassion.\nKnow evil and guard against it, see suffering and feel for it; do not indulge it, and do not hate.\nTo live like this is the Middle Way."]}, "049": {"title": "Is Human Nature Originally Good, Originally Evil, or Does It Turn With Conditions?", "summary": "Not fixing people as good or evil, but observing habit, environment and the choice in this moment.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva:\nQuestion: is human nature originally good, originally evil, or does it turn according to conditions?", "If you say human nature is originally good,\nhow can beings\ngive rise to greed, anger and delusion?\nIf you say human nature is originally evil,\nhow can the wicked\nalso repent in a single thought?\nSo the two words good and evil\ncannot wholly define the hearts of beings.\nThe hearts of beings\narise with conditions,\nmove with habit,\nand turn with what they are steeped in.\nWhen a thought of greed arises,\none may do evil;\nwhen a thought of compassion arises,\none may also do good.\nWhen good thoughts are often cultivated,\ngood roots gradually grow;\nwhen evil thoughts are often practiced,\nevil karma gradually deepens.\nSo today's good person,\nif they do not guard the heart,\nmay also do evil;\ntoday's evil person,\nif they can know their faults,\nmay also turn to good.\nPeople are not born forever good,\nnor born forever evil;\nthe heart arises with conditions,\nand conduct is formed by thought.\nYet you must not therefore say:\n'Since all follows conditions,\ngood and evil have no meaning.'\nPrecisely because the heart can turn,\npractice has meaning.\nIf everything were fixed from birth,\nthe evil forever evil,\nthe good forever good,\nwhat need would there be for teaching?\nWhat need for repentance?\nWhat need for practice?\nBeings have ignorance,\nso they create evil;\nbeings also have the nature of awareness,\nso they can know evil and change.\nWater has no fixed form:\nin a square vessel it is square,\nin a round vessel it is round;\nyet if water is muddied,\nit can be made clear again.\nThe heart is the same.\nThe teaching of the family,\nthe friends one keeps,\nthe surroundings one lives in,\none's own habits,\none's past experiences —\nall can become conditions.\nBut that conditions influence you\ndoes not mean conditions\ndecide everything for you.\nWhen circumstances arrive,\nyou still have one thought\nwith which to choose.\nWhen someone hurts you,\nyou can take revenge,\nor you can stop the evil and not take revenge;\nwhen someone deceives you,\nyou can learn their deceit,\nor you can, because of it,\nkeep faith all the more.\nSo practice is not waiting\nfor good conditions to make you good,\nbut, when conditions appear,\nlearning to choose good.\nIf you ask:\n'Should I then trust human nature?'\nDo not believe that beings\nwill never do evil;\nnor believe that beings\ncan never change.\nKnowing people can do evil,\nyou have the wisdom to protect;\nknowing people can do good,\nyou do not abandon compassion.\nSeeing an evil person,\nguard against their evil,\nwithout cutting off\nthe possibility that they will change;\nseeing a good person,\nrespect their goodness,\nwithout holding them up\nas one who never errs.\nSeeing people this way,\nyou will have less disappointment\nand less rigidity.\nWhat the Dharma values\nis not arguing\nwhether human nature should be labeled\n'good' or 'evil',\nbut asking you:\nthis thought, right now —\nwhere will it go?\nThought after thought\nbecomes habit;\nhabit, over a long time,\nforms the person;\nif a person can become aware,\nhabit too can be turned.\nTherefore I say:\ngood and evil are not set in stone,\nconditions are not fate;\nthe hearts of beings can turn,\nand so beings can be delivered.", "If human nature were originally good, why would beings still give rise to greed, anger and delusion?\nIf human nature were originally evil, why can even wicked people repent in a single moment?\nSo the two words \"good\" and \"evil\" cannot fully define the hearts of beings.\nBeings' hearts arise with conditions, are pulled by habits, and change with the influences they absorb day by day.\nWhen a greedy thought arises, it may lead to a harmful act;\nwhen a compassionate thought arises, it may also lead to a good act.\nWhen good thoughts are cultivated again and again, good roots slowly grow;\nwhen harmful thoughts are repeated again and again, harmful karma gradually deepens.\nSo a good person today, if they do not guard their heart, may also do wrong;\na person who does wrong today, if they can recognize their faults, may also turn toward good.\nPeople are not born forever good, nor born forever evil;\nthe heart moves with conditions, and actions are formed by thoughts.\nBut you cannot therefore say: \"Since everything follows conditions, good and evil have no meaning.\"\nIt is precisely because the heart can change that practice has meaning.\nIf everything were fixed at birth — the wicked forever wicked, the good forever good —\nwhy teach? Why repent? Why practice?\nBecause beings have ignorance, they do wrong;\nbecause beings also have the nature of awareness, they can recognize wrong and change.\nJust as water has no fixed shape — in a square container it becomes square, in a round container it becomes round —\nyet if water is muddied, it can also be made clear again.\nThe heart is like this too.\nFamily upbringing, the friends we keep, the environment we live in, our own habits and our past experiences\ncan all become conditions.\nBut that conditions influence you does not mean they decide everything for you.\nWhen a situation comes, you still have one thought with which to choose.\nWhen someone hurts you, you can choose revenge, or choose to stop the harm and not take revenge;\nwhen someone deceives you, you can learn to deceive others, or become even more trustworthy because of it.\nSo practice is not waiting for good conditions to make you good,\nbut learning to choose good when conditions appear.\nIf you ask: \"Should I trust human nature, then?\"\ndo not believe that beings will never do wrong;\nand do not believe that beings can never change.\nKnowing that people can do wrong, you have the wisdom to protect yourself;\nknowing that people can do good, you do not give up compassion.\nWhen you see someone doing wrong, guard against their wrongdoing, but do not cut off the possibility that they will change;\nwhen you see a good person, respect their goodness, but do not treat them as someone who will never make a mistake.\nSeeing people this way, you will have less disappointment and less rigid prejudice.\nWhat the Dharma values is not arguing over whether human nature should be labeled \"good\" or \"evil\",\nbut asking you:\nwhere will this thought, right now, go?\nOne thought after another becomes habit;\nhabits built up over time form a person's character;\nif a person can become aware, habits can also be changed.\nSo I say:\ngood and evil are not set in stone, and conditions are not fate;\nbeings' hearts can change, and so beings can be delivered."]}, "050": {"title": "What Is Cause and Effect? What Is Karmic Force?", "summary": "Through seeds, soil and daily habits, the author's record on cause and effect and karmic force.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva:\nI humbly ask: what is cause and effect? What is karmic force?", "\"You ask about cause and effect, and also about karmic force.\nFirst know this: cause and effect is not reward and punishment; karmic force is not heaven's decree.\nA thought arising, a word spoken, a deed done — each can become a cause. A cause meets its conditions, and then becomes an effect. It is like a seed placed in soil: the seed is the cause; water, soil and sunlight are its conditions; when cause and conditions are complete, the fruit is born. So where there is a cause, the effect may not be seen at once; and an effect is not formed by a single cause alone. This is called cause and effect.\nWhat is karma? Beings act with intention: the body does, the mouth speaks, the mind thinks; done again and again, this steeps the heart. A thought today seems very small; repeated day after day, it becomes habit. Habit then makes it easier for similar thoughts and similar deeds to arise. This continuing momentum may be called karmic force.\nKarmic force is not a pair of hands punishing you in the dark; it is what beings have done, leaving behind a force that in turn influences later conditions.\nOne who is often angry grows ever more practiced in anger; even at small things, they flare up easily. One who often deceives grows ever deeper in the habit of deceit; when gain and loss are at stake, they easily deceive again. One who is often kind grows ever more practiced in kindness; seeing beings suffer, they easily reach out a hand. So karma is formed by deeds, and deeds are in turn steeped in karma.\nYet you must understand: to be influenced by karma is not to be locked in by karma. If karma could not be turned, practice would have no meaning; if effects could not be changed, repentance would be useless. Precisely because conditions are not yet fixed, a single thought today can still add new causes. Causes already planted in the past cannot be made never to have happened; but today you can change their conditions, and you can plant new causes as well.\nIt is like a field that already holds the seeds of weeds: you cannot say the seeds never fell; but you can weed, improve the soil, water it, and plant good seedlings. Over time, the field will look different. This is practice.\nSo do not, whenever you meet suffering, ask: 'What evil karma did I create?'\nA single matter in the world is usually the coming together of many conditions. Illness has bodily causes, accidents have practical causes, and harm done by others also comes from their own choices. Do not, whenever hardship comes, blame it all on karmic retribution from past lives. That is not understanding cause and effect; it is being deluded about cause and effect.\nCause and effect teaches people to take responsibility, not to blame themselves; karmic force teaches people to be careful in what they do, not to resign themselves to fate. What you can hold on to today is this very moment. When an evil thought arises, know it and do not follow it; when harsh words are about to come out, know it and be able to stop; when a good thought arises, let it become action. Each time is small, but over time it runs deep. So do not belittle a single good thought, nor belittle a single evil thought.\nCause and effect lets people know: what is done is not in vain.\nKarmic force lets people know: what is practiced becomes momentum.\nPractice lets people know: though momentum is formed, it can still change direction.\nIf you understand this, you need not frighten yourself with cause and effect, nor use karmic force as an excuse for your choices. Yesterday's karma becomes one of today's conditions; today's heart becomes one of tomorrow's causes.\nTherefore I say:\ncause and effect is not a verdict; karmic force is not a shackle.\nKnowing cause and effect, be careful what you plant;\nknowing karmic force, be careful what you practice;\nknowing dependent arising, know that things can turn;\nknowing they can turn, do not despair.\"", "You ask about cause and effect, and also about karmic force.\nFirst you should know: cause and effect is not reward and punishment, and karmic force is not destiny decreed by heaven.\nA thought that arises, a word that is spoken, a deed that is done — each can become a \"cause\". A cause meets its \"conditions\", and only then does it form an \"effect\".\nIt is like a seed buried in the soil: the seed is the cause; soil, water and sunlight are the conditions; only when cause and conditions are complete does fruit appear.\nSo having a cause does not mean you will see the result right away; and a result is not produced by just one single cause. This is cause and effect.\nWhat is karma?\nBeings act intentionally — the body acts, the mouth speaks, the mind thinks — and doing this over and over shapes their own hearts.\nOne thought today looks very small; but repeated every day, it becomes a habit. Habits then make it easier for similar thoughts and similar behavior to arise. This continuing force is called karmic force.\nKarmic force is not a pair of hands secretly punishing you; it is the force left behind by what beings themselves have done, which in turn affects the conditions that follow.\nWhen a person is often angry, their anger becomes more and more practiced; even small things easily set them off. When a person often lies, the habit of lying grows deeper; when gain or loss is at stake, they easily lie again. When a person is often kind, their kindness becomes more and more practiced; when they see others suffering, they easily reach out to help.\nSo karma is formed by actions, and actions in turn are shaped by karma.\nHowever, you must understand: being influenced by karma does not mean being locked in by karma.\nIf karma could not change at all, practice would have no meaning; if results could not change at all, repentance would be useless.\nIt is precisely because conditions are not yet fixed that a single thought today can still add a new cause.\nCauses already planted in the past cannot be made to have never happened; but today you can change their conditions, and you can also plant new causes.\nFor example, if a field already has weed seeds in it, you cannot say the seeds never fell; but you can pull the weeds, improve the soil, water it, and plant good seedlings. Over time, the field will look different. This is practice.\nSo do not, whenever you meet hardship, ask: \"What bad karma did I create?\" Things in the world usually come from many conditions together. Illness has bodily causes, accidents have practical causes, and when others hurt you, that also comes from their own choices. You must not blame every hardship on retribution from past lives. That is not understanding cause and effect, but being confused about it.\nCause and effect teaches people to take responsibility, not to blame themselves; karmic force teaches people to be careful in what they do, not to accept fate passively.\nWhat you can hold on to today is this moment. When a bad thought arises, notice it and do not follow it; when harsh words are about to come out, notice it and be able to stop; when a good thought arises, let it become action. Each time is small, but over time it goes deep.\nSo do not underestimate one good thought, and do not underestimate one bad thought.\nCause and effect lets people know that what we do is never in vain. Karmic force lets people know that what we repeatedly practice becomes a force. Practice lets people know that even though a force has formed, it can still change direction.\nIf you understand this, you will not need to frighten yourself with cause and effect, and you must not use karmic force as an excuse for your own choices.\nYesterday's karma becomes one of today's conditions; today's heart becomes one of tomorrow's causes.\nSo I say:\ncause and effect is not a verdict, and karmic force is not a shackle. Knowing cause and effect, be careful what you plant; knowing karmic force, be careful what you repeat; knowing dependent arising, know that things can change; knowing they can change, do not despair."]}, "051": {"title": "Compassion Need Not Mean Closeness; Filial Piety Can Have Boundaries", "summary": "When family relationships cause harm, asking about filial piety, distance and protecting oneself.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva:\n\"We often hear of children who do not get along with their parents — children mocked, scolded and verbally controlled by their parents for years, or who later become unfilial because of it.\nFrom the Buddhist point of view, is this the past-life karma of parent and child (collecting and repaying debts)?\nWhen facing parents who are our own flesh and blood, if their words and deeds have seriously harmed their children, and the children, to protect themselves, choose to keep their distance and step away where they should — under the moral burden and guilt of traditional filial piety, how can they settle their hearts, so as to have the wisdom to protect themselves without losing the compassion of a son or daughter?\"", "The bond between parents and children is very deep.\nYet a deep bond is not always a good bond;\nbeing of one blood does not mean never wounding each other.\nPeople often say:\n\"The child comes to collect a debt, the parent comes to repay it; what was owed in a past life is repaid in this one.\"\nThis saying may serve as a metaphor; it must not be clung to as a fixed conclusion.\nSince you do not know your past lives, do not use the suffering of this life to judge rashly the sins of a past one.\nWhen parents hurt their child, do not simply say:\n\"This is the child's debt from a past life, so it must be borne.\"\nWhen a child resents their parents, neither simply say:\n\"This is the parents' past-life karma; they deserve it in this life.\"\nTo speak of cause and effect like this easily uses its name to justify today's harm.\nOf causes unknown, make no fixed judgment;\nevil before your eyes should be stopped before your eyes.\nIf parents harm their children over a long time with rage, humiliation and control,\nif their conduct is not good, then it is not good.\nDo not, because they are parents, rename harm as loving kindness.\nNor, because a child suffers, demand of them:\n\"This is your karma; you should endure it.\"\nPatience is not letting others harm you;\nfilial piety is not losing all boundaries;\ncompassion is not indulging evil deeds.\nIf drawing near only deepens anger on both sides;\nif every meeting stirs up harsh words again;\nif living together long has left body and mind without peace,\nthen stepping away from that situation for a time is not necessarily unfilial.\nTo distance yourself from a harmful bond is not to cut off a kind heart.\nYou may see them less, without cursing;\nyou may refuse, without humiliating;\nyou may set boundaries, without revenge;\nyou may no longer bear their control, and still wish them peace.\nThis is: the body may keep a distance; the heart need not hold hatred.\nIf your parents are in need and it is within your means,\nand does not throw you back into harm, you may care for them in measure.\nIf meeting them in person would surely bring great suffering, you may find another way;\nyou need not bear everything yourself.\nFilial piety must have wisdom to last.\nTrue filial piety is not children obeying whatever parents say.\nIf parents act badly, for the child to advise them gently is also filial;\nif they do not listen, keep to what is right for yourself,\nand do not, in order to obey your parents, end up doing wrong as well.\nIf your heart is filled with guilt:\n\"They are my parents after all — how can I keep away?\"\nask yourself:\n\"Am I abandoning my parents, or am I stopping us from going on hurting each other?\"\nThe two are not the same.\nSome distance is not heartlessness;\nrather, it keeps both sides from creating harsh speech, anger and new grievances day after day.\nIf stepping back a little means each creates a little less harm,\nthis distance may also be a skillful means.\nYet you should also reflect on yourself:\ndo not, because you were hurt, make revenge your boundary;\ndo not, because your parents erred, judge their whole life as evil.\nWhere they were wrong, see that it was wrong; where there was kindness, know it as kindness.\nThat there was kindness does not mean the harm did not exist;\nthat there was harm need not erase all the kindness that once was.\nWhen you can see both at once, the heart gradually leaves the two extremes of love and hate.\nAs for so-called past-life cause and effect, if you believe in it, you may understand it this way:\nsince you have met today, there are today's conditions.\nAs for who owed whom how much in a past life, do not trouble to calculate.\nWhat truly matters is: in this life, do not keep running up debts to each other.\nWhen parents hurt their children with harsh words, it should stop;\nwhen children retaliate with harsh words, it too should stop.\nIf one side cannot change, the other can still decide\nnot to let harmful karma continue through themselves.\nThis is where karma is turned.\nSo I advise the wounded children:\nyou need not prove your pain through hatred;\nnor prove your filial piety by sacrificing yourself.\nWhat needs healing, heal;\nwhere you need help, seek help;\nwhere boundaries are needed, set boundaries;\nwhere you should step away, you may step away.\nBut if one day your heart is strong enough and you are willing to slowly put down resentment,\nit is not because everything your parents did was right,\nbut because you do not want past wounds to rule the rest of your life.\nIf there is forgiveness, it must arise from the heart; it cannot be forced.\nIf you cannot yet forgive, first do not retaliate;\nif you cannot yet be close, first keep your distance;\nif you cannot yet let go, first protect yourself.\nStep by step — this too is practice.\nSo you ask: how to have the wisdom to protect yourself without losing a child's compassion?\nI say:\ncompassion need not mean closeness; filial piety can have boundaries.\nNot indulging their evil is wisdom;\nnot wishing them to suffer is compassion;\nnot repaying evil with evil is practice;\nnot letting yourself be hurt again is also protecting life.\nDo not use \"filial piety\" to force people to bear harm;\ndo not use \"cause and effect\" to find reasons for harm.\nIf you ask about past lives, past lives are hard to know;\nif you ask about today, today the mutual wounding can stop.\nCause and effect does not tell you to endure evil;\nit reminds you: do not let evil go on through yourself.", "The bond between parents and children is very deep. But a deep bond does not necessarily mean a good one; being related by blood does not mean you will never hurt each other.\nPeople often say: \"Children come to collect debts, parents come to repay them; what was owed in a past life is repaid in this one.\" This can be used as a metaphor, but it must not be held as a fixed conclusion. Since you do not know what happened in past lives, do not use this life's pain to rashly judge past-life wrongs.\nWhen parents hurt their children, you cannot just say: \"The children owed this from a past life, so they should bear it.\" When children resent their parents, you cannot just say: \"The parents created this karma in a past life, so they deserve it now.\" Talking about cause and effect this way easily uses its name to justify today's harm.\nDo not draw conclusions about causes you cannot know; harm happening in front of you should be stopped right there.\nIf parents hurt their children over a long time through angry scolding, humiliation and control, then if that behavior is not good, it is not good. You cannot rename harm as love just because they are parents. Nor can you demand of a suffering child: \"This is your karma, you should endure it.\"\nPatience is not letting others hurt you; filial piety is not losing your boundaries; compassion is not indulging harmful behavior.\nIf being close only makes the anger between you deeper; if every meeting brings harsh words again; if being together for a long time has already left your body and mind without peace — then leaving that environment for a while is not necessarily unfilial. Staying away from a harmful bond is not cutting off your kindness.\nYou can see them less, without cursing them; you can say no, without humiliating them; you can set boundaries, without seeking revenge; you can stop accepting their control and still wish them well. That is: the body can keep a distance, and the heart need not hold hatred.\nIf your parents need something, you can help to a reasonable degree, as long as it is within your means and does not put you back in harm's way. If direct contact will certainly cause great pain, you can find other ways; you do not have to bear everything in person. Filial piety needs wisdom in order to last.\nTrue filial piety is not children obeying everything their parents say. If parents behave badly, gently advising them is also filial; if they do not listen, hold on to what is right for you, and do not, for the sake of obeying your parents, end up doing wrong yourself.\nIf guilt arises in your heart — \"They are my parents after all, how can I keep away?\" — ask yourself: \"Am I abandoning my parents, or am I stopping us from continuing to hurt each other?\" These two are not the same.\nSome distance is not coldness; instead, it keeps both sides from creating harsh speech, anger and new resentments every day. If stepping back a little lets both of you do a little less harm, that distance can also be a skillful means.\nBut you should also look at yourself: do not make revenge your boundary because you were hurt; do not judge your parents' whole life as bad because they made mistakes. Where they were wrong, see that it was wrong; where they were kind, recognize their kindness. Having been kind does not mean the harm did not exist; having caused harm does not have to erase all the kindness there once was. When you can see both at the same time, the heart gradually leaves the two extremes of love and hate.\nAs for so-called past-life cause and effect, if you believe in it, you can understand it like this: since you have met today, there are today's conditions. As for who owed whom how much in a past life, do not bother calculating. What truly matters is: in this life, do not keep running up debts to each other.\nWhen parents hurt their children with harsh words, it should stop; when children strike back with harsh words, that should stop too. If one side cannot change, the other can still decide not to let harmful karma continue through themselves. This is where karma is turned around.\nSo I advise children who have been hurt: you do not need hatred to prove your pain; nor do you need to sacrifice yourself to prove your filial piety. What needs healing, get it healed; where you need help, ask for help; where boundaries are needed, set them; where you need to leave, you may leave.\nBut if one day your heart has enough strength and you are willing to slowly put down your resentment, it is not because everything your parents did was right, but because you do not want past wounds to keep ruling the rest of your life.\nIf forgiveness comes, it has to come from the heart; it cannot be forced. If you cannot forgive yet, first do not retaliate; if you cannot be close yet, first keep your distance; if you cannot let go yet, first take good care of yourself. Step by step — this too is practice.\nSo you ask: how can I have the wisdom to protect myself without losing a child's compassion? I say:\ncompassion need not mean closeness; filial piety can have boundaries. Not indulging their wrongdoing is wisdom; not wishing them to suffer is compassion; not repaying harm with harm is practice; not letting yourself be hurt again is also protecting life.\nDo not use \"filial piety\" to force people to bear harm; do not use \"cause and effect\" to find excuses for harm. If you ask about past lives, they are hard to know; if you ask about today, today the mutual hurting can stop.\nCause and effect does not tell you to endure harm; it reminds you not to let harm continue through yourself."]}, "052": {"title": "Is \"Living in the Present\" Just a Comforting Phrase?", "summary": "The past can be learned from and the future prepared for; bring the step you can take back into this moment.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva:\nIs \"living in the present\" just a phrase to comfort people?", "\"You ask:\n'Is living in the present\nonly a phrase of comfort?'\nI say:\nif it only hangs on the lips,\nit is indeed just a phrase;\nif it can be practiced in the heart,\nit is a discipline.\nAmong the sufferings of beings,\nsome are suffered today;\nyet many sufferings\ncome from yesterday, already gone,\nand tomorrow, not yet come.\nInsulted yesterday,\ntoday one relives the insult again and again;\ntomorrow has not arrived,\nyet today one already fears it.\nA thing happens only once,\nbut the heart makes it\nhappen a hundred times.\nSo what is called the present\ndoes not tell you\nto forget the past,\nnor tell you\nnot to think of the future.\nRather:\nthe past is for understanding,\nnot for living in;\nthe future is for preparing,\nnot for suffering in advance.\nSome say:\n'If we live in the present,\nwhy plan for tomorrow?'\nThis is not the present.\nSaving money today\nis today's deed;\nstudying today\nis today's deed;\ntreating an illness today\nis today's deed;\npreparing today for tomorrow\nis still acting in the present.\nWhat truly takes people out of the present\nis not planning the future,\nbut a heart that has already run off\ninto things that have not happened,\nfearing them over and over.\nOthers say:\n'If we live in the present,\ndo not think about the past.'\nNor is this so.\nIf there were faults in the past,\nreflect on them;\nif there were wounds in the past,\nheal them;\nif there was kindness in the past,\nremember it with gratitude.\nBut do not let yesterday\nreplay every day,\nuntil today too\nbecomes yesterday's prisoner.\nRemembering\nand being trapped\nare not the same thing.\nIf you were once hurt by someone,\nliving in the present\nis not telling yourself:\n'It's nothing,\nit's all over.'\nIt is knowing:\n'It truly happened,\nand I was truly hurt.\nBut the me of today\ncan decide\nhow to take the next step.'\nThis\nis not escape,\nbut taking back this moment.\nIf you worry about the future,\nneither is it telling yourself:\n'Don't think about anything;\nthings will work themselves out.'\nRather, ask:\n'Right now, what is there\nthat I can do?'\nWhat can be done,\ndo;\nwhat cannot be done,\nset aside for now.\nWisdom is not\nhaving no worries;\nit is knowing\nwhat can be dealt with now,\nand what can only wait for now.\nBeings often think:\nwhen I have money,\nI will be at peace;\nwhen I have a family,\nI will be at peace;\nwhen this is settled,\nI will be at peace;\nwhen all my problems are solved,\nthen I will truly live.\nYet as soon as one thing ends,\nanother arises.\nIf you must wait\nuntil life has no problems\nbefore allowing yourself to rest,\nthen in a whole lifetime,\nwhat day will you ever live?\nSo practicing the present\nis not because\nthe future does not matter;\nit is precisely because life is impermanent\nthat today matters.\nWhen you eat,\nknow that you are eating;\nwhen you are with others,\nknow that you are together now;\nwhen the heart is sad,\nknow that you are sad;\nwhen the heart is glad,\nknow that you are glad.\nNot every moment\nneeds to be happy;\nonly let every moment\nnot deceive your own heart.\nThe present is not happiness;\nthe present is truth.\nIf there is suffering today,\ndeal with today's suffering;\nif there is joy today,\nknow that this joy too is impermanent.\nNot chasing,\nnot refusing,\nknowing it comes,\nand knowing it goes —\nthis is cultivating the heart.\nTherefore I say:\nliving in the present\nis not comforting you that\n'everything will get better';\nit is reminding you:\nwhether the future is good or bad,\nthe only place you can truly set your hand\nis always now.\nYesterday cannot be changed,\ntomorrow has not come;\nbut a thought this moment,\na word this moment,\na deed this moment\nis becoming\nthe cause of the next.\nSo the principle of cause and effect\ntoo does not leave the present.\nIf you ask:\n'Life holds so much suffering —\nhow can I live in the present?'\nYou need not demand of yourself\nthat you always stay settled.\nWhen the mind runs off,\nknow that it has run off,\nand come back.\nWhen it runs off again,\nknow it again,\nand come back again.\nIt is not never being scattered,\nbut each time you are scattered,\nstill knowing to return.\nThat is all.\nDo not take this lightly.\nA lifetime\nseems to be decades;\nwhat is truly lived\nhas only ever been\none present moment after another.\nTherefore:\nthe past can be learned from,\nnot dwelt in;\nthe future can be prepared for,\nnot clung to;\nthe present can be acted in,\nnot lost.\nThis is not a phrase of comfort.\nIt is the only place\nwhere beings can truly\nset their hearts, choose,\nand change.\"", "You ask:\n\"Is living in the present just a phrase to comfort people?\"\nI say:\nif it only stays on the lips, it really is just a phrase;\nbut if it can be put into practice in the heart, it becomes a real discipline.\nSome of beings' suffering is happening right now;\nbut much of it comes from yesterday, which has already passed,\nand from tomorrow, which has not yet arrived.\nYou were insulted yesterday, and today you keep enduring that insult again and again;\ntomorrow has not come, and today you are already afraid of it.\nSomething happened only once, but the mind makes it happen a hundred times.\nSo living in the present does not mean forgetting the past, nor not thinking about the future.\nIt means:\nthe past is for understanding, not for living in;\nthe future is for preparing, not for suffering in advance.\nSome people say:\n\"If we live in the present, why plan for tomorrow?\"\nThat is not the present.\nSaving money today is today's action;\nstudying today is today's action;\ngetting treatment today is today's action;\npreparing today for tomorrow is still acting in the present.\nWhat truly takes people out of the present is not planning for the future,\nbut the mind running off into things that have not happened yet and fearing them again and again.\nOthers say:\n\"If we live in the present, we shouldn't think about the past.\"\nThat is not right either.\nIf there were mistakes in the past, reflect on them;\nif there were wounds, heal them;\nif there was kindness, be grateful for it.\nBut do not let yesterday replay every day,\nuntil today also becomes yesterday's prisoner.\nRemembering and being trapped are two different things.\nIf you were once hurt by someone,\nliving in the present is not telling yourself:\n\"It's fine, it's all over.\"\nIt is knowing:\n\"It really happened, and I really was hurt.\nBut the me of today can decide how to take the next step.\"\nThis is not running away, but taking back this moment.\nIf you are worried about the future,\nit is also not telling yourself:\n\"Don't think about it; it will all work out somehow.\"\nInstead, ask:\n\"Right now, what can I do?\"\nWhat you can do, do;\nwhat you cannot do, set aside for now.\nWisdom is not having no worries;\nit is knowing what can be handled now and what can only wait for now.\nPeople often think:\nonce I have money, I'll be at ease;\nonce I have a family, I'll be at ease;\nonce this is settled, I'll be at ease;\nonce all my problems are solved, then I'll really live.\nBut as soon as one thing is finished, another comes along.\nIf you must wait until life has no problems before you let yourself be at peace,\nthen over a whole lifetime, when will you ever really live?\nSo practicing the present is not because the future is unimportant;\nit is precisely because life is impermanent that today matters.\nWhen you eat, know that you are eating;\nwhen you are with others, know that you are together right now;\nwhen you are sad, know that you are sad;\nwhen you are happy, know that you are happy.\nNot every moment has to be happy;\nyou only need to not deceive your own heart in each moment.\nThe present is not happiness; the present is what is real.\nIf there is suffering today, deal with today's suffering;\nif there is joy today, know that this joy is impermanent too.\nNot chasing, not resisting; knowing when it comes, and knowing when it goes.\nThis is cultivating the heart.\nSo I say:\nliving in the present is not comforting you that \"everything will be fine\";\nit is reminding you:\nwhether the future turns out well or badly, the only place you can truly start is always now.\nYesterday cannot be changed, tomorrow has not come;\nbut a thought in this moment, a word in this moment, an action in this moment\nis becoming the cause of the next moment.\nSo the principle of cause and effect is not separate from the present either.\nIf you ask:\n\"Life has so much suffering — how can I live in the present?\"\nYou do not need to demand that you always stay settled.\nWhen the mind wanders off, notice that it has wandered, and bring it back.\nWhen it wanders off again, notice again, and bring it back again.\nIt is not about never being distracted, but about always knowing to come back each time you are.\nThat is all it is.\nDo not underestimate this.\nA lifetime looks like several decades;\nbut what is truly lived has only ever been one present moment after another.\nTherefore:\nthe past can be learned from, but not stayed in;\nthe future can be prepared for, but not clung to;\nthe present can be acted in, but must not be missed.\nThese are not words of comfort.\nThis is the only place where beings can truly set their intention, choose, and change."]}, "053": {"title": "Is Reciting a Sutra in Vain If You Don't Finish It? One Teaching That Freed Countless People From Self-Blame", "summary": "When sutra recitation must pause, how to settle self-blame and carry on with reverence and a good heart.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva:\n\"When reciting a sutra and something suddenly comes up halfway — a phone call, someone coming to see me — so that I have to put the sutra down, is the recitation in vain? Please, compassionate Bodhisattva, give a teaching.\"", "You ask: if a sutra recitation is not finished\nand something suddenly arises,\nso that you must stop,\nhas everything recited before\nbeen wholly in vain?\nI say:\na sutra not recited to the end\ndoes not mean its merit is lost;\na moment's interruption\ndoes not mean all prior effort is wasted.\nThe merit of recitation\ndoes not lie only\nin one scroll from beginning to end.\nIt lies also\nin the reverent heart,\nthe benefit of hearing the Dharma,\nand the practice of gathering the mind.\nIf in reciting one line\nyou give rise to a measure of reverence;\nif in reading one verse\nyou understand a measure of the Dharma's meaning —\nthough unfinished,\ncan this good heart and good deed\nbe said to be nothing?\nThe Dharma values the intention,\nand also values practice.\nIf, while reciting,\na phone call suddenly comes,\nsomeone visits,\nor urgent matters must be handled,\nfirst discern what weighs more.\nIf the matter is not urgent,\nyou may attend to it after finishing;\nif the matter is pressing,\nyou may pause to deal with it.\nDo not, by clinging to\nthe idea that a scroll must be finished,\nlose the compassion you ought to have.\nIf your parents have an urgent need,\nyour children are in danger,\nor someone is waiting for help,\nand you say:\n'My sutra is not finished;\nI cannot stop,'\nthen ask:\nare you cultivating compassion,\nor clinging to form?\nReciting sutras is to cultivate the heart,\nnot to bind it.\nThe sutras teach beings compassion;\nif because of recitation\nyou ignore the suffering before your eyes,\nyou should look again\nat the meaning of practice.\nYet neither should this\ngive rise to laziness.\nDo not, because anything may interrupt,\nlet your recitation\nconstantly stop at every distraction.\nWhen you can focus,\nfocus;\nwhen you must attend to matters,\nattend to them.\nPausing for a matter\ndoes not lose reverence;\nresuming when it is done\nis also diligence.\nIf you must leave midway,\nyou may place the sutra\nin a clean and suitable place,\nkeeping in mind\nwhere you paused.\nWhen the matter is done,\nif time allows,\ncontinue from where you stopped.\nIf you wish to begin again from the start,\nthat too is fine.\nIf today there is truly no time,\ncontinue tomorrow,\nwithout blaming yourself for it.\nAs for dedicating merit,\nyou may dedicate it after finishing a section,\naccording to your own practice;\nif you could not finish because of a matter,\nyou may still dedicate\nthe good you have cultivated.\nThe heart of dedication\nneed not wait for a scroll to be complete\nbefore it can arise.\nIf you ask:\n'If I do not finish,\nwill the Bodhisattva be displeased?'\nI urge you:\ndo not treat recitation\nas delivering a quota\nto the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas.\nNor think\nthat reciting one line less\nleaves you in debt.\nThe Dharma is not a transaction;\npractice is not counting.\nNumbers can aid diligence;\nthey must not become fear.\nIf after finishing a scroll\nthe heart grows proud,\nyou should still watch it;\nif half a scroll is left unfinished\nbut the heart holds reverence,\nit is not in vain.\nWhat truly matters\nis whether the Dharma in the sutra\nis gradually entering your heart.\nReciting the Ksitigarbha Sutra,\nlearn its compassionate vows;\nreciting the Diamond Sutra,\nlearn to leave behind appearances;\nreciting the Universal Gate Chapter,\nlearn compassion.\nIf you know the text well\nyet still often flare up in anger when things happen,\nthen reflect\nwhether the meaning you recite\nis being used in your life.\nIf, during recitation,\nsomeone comes to see you\nand you can respond gently,\nnot annoyed at the interruption,\nthis too is using the sutra's meaning\nin the present.\nTherefore I say:\nan interrupted recitation\nis not reading in vain;\nonce a good thought has arisen,\nit is not emptied by a pause.\nIf you can continue, continue;\nif you cannot, be at peace;\nlose no reverence,\ngive rise to no fear.\nDo not cling to\nwhether the text is complete,\nand forget\nwhat practice was for in the first place.\nThe sutra can pause;\nthe good heart must not be forgotten.\nThe words can be set down;\nthe Dharma can still be lived.", "You ask: if a sutra recitation is not yet finished and something suddenly comes up so that you have to stop, is everything recited before wasted?\nI say:\nnot finishing a sutra does not mean all the merit disappears;\nstopping for a moment does not mean all the earlier effort was wasted.\nThe merit of reciting is not only in finishing a whole scroll from beginning to end.\nIt is also in the reverence of the heart as you recite, the benefit of hearing the Dharma, and the practice of gathering the mind.\nIf reciting one line brings forth a little reverence,\nif reading one verse helps you understand a little of the Dharma,\nthen even if you have not finished, how could this good heart and good deed be nothing at all?\nThe Dharma values intention, and it also values putting it into practice.\nIf, while you are reciting, the phone suddenly rings, someone visits, or something urgent must be handled,\nfirst sort out what is more important.\nIf it is not urgent, you can deal with it after you finish;\nif it is important, you can pause and take care of it.\nDo not cling to \"I must finish this scroll\" and lose the compassion you should have.\nIf your parents urgently need you, your children are in danger, or someone is waiting for help,\nand you say: \"I haven't finished my sutra, I can't stop,\"\nthen ask yourself: are you practicing compassion, or clinging to form?\nReciting sutras is meant to cultivate the heart, not to tie it up.\nThe sutras teach beings compassion; if reciting makes you ignore the suffering in front of you,\nyou should look again at what practice is for.\nBut do not let this make you lazy either.\nDo not think everything is a reason to stop, so that your recitation keeps breaking off whenever your mind wanders.\nWhen you can focus, focus; when you need to handle something, handle it.\nPausing because something comes up does not lose reverence;\ncontinuing after it is done is also diligence.\nIf you have to leave in the middle, you can place the sutra book somewhere clean and suitable,\nand remember where you stopped.\nWhen you have finished, if time allows, continue from where you left off.\nIf you would like to start again from the beginning, that is fine too.\nIf you really have no time today, continue tomorrow; there is no need to blame yourself.\nAs for dedicating merit, you can dedicate it after finishing a section, following your own practice;\nif you could not finish because something came up, you can still dedicate the good you have already done.\nThe wish to dedicate merit does not have to wait until a whole scroll is complete.\nIf you ask: \"If I don't finish, will the Bodhisattva blame me?\"\nI urge you: do not treat sutra recitation as handing in a quota to the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas.\nAnd do not think that reciting one line less means you owe something.\nThe Dharma is not a transaction, and practice is not counting.\nNumbers can help you be diligent, but they must not turn into fear.\nIf you finish a whole scroll and become proud, you should still watch your heart;\nif you have not finished half a scroll but your heart is reverent, it is not in vain.\nWhat really matters is whether the Dharma in the sutra is gradually entering your heart.\nWhen reciting the Ksitigarbha Sutra, learn its compassionate vows;\nwhen reciting the Diamond Sutra, learn to let go of appearances;\nwhen reciting the Universal Gate Chapter, learn compassion.\nIf you know the text by heart but still often get angry when things happen,\nreflect on whether the meaning you recite is being used in your daily life.\nIf someone comes to find you in the middle of reciting and you can respond gently, without getting annoyed at the interruption,\nthat too is using the meaning of the sutra in the present moment.\nSo I say:\nan interrupted recitation is not wasted reading;\nonce a good thought has arisen, it does not vanish because of a pause.\nIf you can continue, continue; if you cannot, be at peace;\nkeep your reverence, and do not give rise to fear.\nDo not cling to whether the text is complete and forget what practice was for in the first place.\nThe sutra can pause, but the good heart must not be forgotten;\nthe words can be put down, but the Dharma can still be lived."]}, "054": {"title": "Not Speaking Up When You See Wrong — Compassion or Cowardice?", "summary": "What we disagree with is not always evil, and silence is not always wrong; first tell difference from harm, advise without forcing, and let compassion never indulge evil.", "blocks": ["I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva:\n\"If a friend does something I do not agree with, but I do not stop them or advise them, is this wrong? Please, compassionate Bodhisattva, give a teaching.\"", "Good man,\nyou ask whether, when a friend's conduct\ndoes not accord with your own mind,\nand you neither stop them\nnor advise them,\nyou fail in compassion,\nor are at fault.\nI now tell you:\nwhat you disagree with is not necessarily evil;\nnot advising is not necessarily wrong.\nBeings in the world\neach have their own views,\ntheir own habits,\ntheir own conditions.\nWhat you delight in,\nthey may not delight in;\nwhat you believe,\nthey may not believe.\nIf whenever something does not suit you\nyou wish to make others change it,\nthis is not compassion;\nit may be self-attachment.\nSo first ask:\nis this matter truly not good,\nor only different from my own view?\nIf it is only a difference of temperament,\nof choices,\nof ways of living,\nharming neither oneself\nnor others,\nthen there is no need to advise on everything.\nTo accept that others are different\nis also practice.\nViews may differ;\ncompassion need not lessen because of it.\nYet if what your friend does\ntruly harms others —\ndeceiving, seizing,\nor doing all manner of unwholesome things —\nand you know it is not right,\nand have the opportunity to advise them,\nthen you should consider\nhow, with skillful means,\nto help them see their fault.\nAt such a time, if you seek only\nto preserve appearances,\nknowing someone is being harmed\nyet paying it no heed at all,\nthen reflect\nwhether, out of fear,\nindifference, or attachment to the relationship,\nyou have abandoned the good you ought to do.\nYet I also tell you:\nencouraging others toward good\ndoes not mean you must argue with them;\nseeing someone at fault\ndoes not mean you must stop them by force.\nSome people\nwake at a single word;\nsome people\nheed not a hundred words.\nSometimes\nit is fitting to speak directly;\nsometimes\none must wait for the right conditions.\nSo before advising someone,\nobserve four things:\nIs there truly a fault in the matter?\nAre your words true to the facts?\nIs the time suitable?\nDoes your heart come from compassion?\nIf the words are true\nbut carry anger,\neven good words can\nbecome a weapon that wounds.\nIf the words are gentle\nbut indulge harm,\neven gentleness may not\nbe true compassion.\nTruthful without harsh speech;\ncompassionate without indulgence.\nIf you see a friend at fault,\nyou may first advise them privately;\nthere is no need to rebuke them in public\nand make them lose face.\nWhen you advise,\ndo not point out their faults\nwith a heart that thinks itself above them.\nWith a heart of equality,\ntell them what is at stake.\nYou may say:\n'I may not fully understand,\nbut I fear this may cause harm;\nI hope you will think it over again.'\nIf they are willing to listen,\nexplain it kindly;\nif they are not willing,\nthere is no need to press them in anger.\nAdvising is your goodwill;\nchanging is their choice.\nYet this too has its limits.\nIf someone before your eyes is being harmed,\nthe situation is urgent,\nand you are able to help safely,\nyou must not use\n'each has their own karma'\nas a reason to stand by with folded arms.\nIf you can stop it safely,\nstop it;\nif you can seek help,\nseek help;\nif you cannot handle it yourself,\nyou can find the right people\nto help stop the harm.\nRespecting others' choices\nis not letting others do harm.\nIf a friend, advised again and again, will not listen\nand still does what is unwholesome,\nyou should also know:\ncompassion can always remain,\nand the friendship can have boundaries.\nYou need not, because of years of friendship,\ncover up their wrongs;\nnor need you, because they erred,\nhate them forever.\nDo not aid their evil,\nnor abandon their good;\ndo not indulge their conduct,\nnor hate the person.\nYou should also look at your own heart.\nSometimes not advising\nis because of wisdom,\nknowing this is not the moment to speak.\nSometimes not advising\nis because of fear,\nafraid of losing a friend.\nSometimes not advising\nis because of indifference,\nthinking others' suffering\nhas nothing to do with you.\nOutwardly it is the same silence;\ninwardly it is different.\nSo do not only ask:\n'I did not advise —\nwas that wrong?'\nAsk further:\n'Why did I not advise?\nDid I know clearly there was harm?\nWas I able to help?\nDid my silence\nlet the harm continue?'\nThe same silence\nmay be wisdom,\nor may be cowardice;\nyou must look at the heart,\nand also at the result.\nGood man,\npractice is not\nremaking all your friends\ninto people you approve of.\nNor is it,\non seeing what is unwholesome,\nalways keeping silent.\nTrue compassion\nis sometimes gentle advice;\nsometimes quiet listening;\nsometimes respecting differences;\nsometimes firmly stopping evil.\nCompassion must have wisdom;\nwisdom must not be without compassion.\nIf you have done all your goodwill can do\nand they still will not change,\nyou need not carry all of their consequences\nupon yourself.\nYou can advise on the causes,\nbut cannot act in their place;\nyou can show them the road,\nbut cannot walk it for them.\nBut if beings are still being harmed,\nconsider what reasonable, safe help\nyou may still offer.\nFinally, I tell you in four lines:\nSeeing difference, there is no need to contend;\nseeing evil, consider how to advise;\nadvise without forcing;\nbe compassionate without indulging evil.\nAnd also:\nDo not bind others with your own views;\ndo not cover their faults out of friendship;\nadvise where you can, stop where you must;\ndo all the good you can, and do not cling to the result.", "Good man,\nyou ask: when a friend does something that does not sit right with you,\nif you neither stop them nor advise them,\nare you failing in compassion? Are you at fault?\nI tell you now:\nwhat you do not agree with is not necessarily evil; not giving advice is not necessarily wrong.\nThe beings of this world\neach have their own views,\ntheir own habits,\nand their own conditions.\nWhat you like, the other person may not like;\nwhat you believe, the other person may not believe.\nIf you want people to change whenever something does not suit you,\nthat is not compassion; it may be self-attachment.\nSo first ask:\nis this thing actually not good in itself, or is it just different from my view?\nIf it is only a difference of personality,\na different choice,\na different way of living,\nand it hurts neither themselves nor anyone else,\nthen there is no need to give advice about everything.\nBeing able to accept that others are different from you is also a kind of practice.\nViews can differ; compassion need not shrink because of it.\nBut if what your friend does truly hurts others —\nsuch as deceiving, taking what is not theirs, or doing other harmful things —\nand you know it is not right and have the chance to advise them,\nthen you should think about how to use skillful means to help them see their mistake.\nAt such a time, if you only care about keeping things pleasant,\nknowing someone is being harmed and ignoring it completely,\nthen you should reflect: is it because of fear, indifference, or clinging to the relationship that you have given up the good you should do?\nBut I also tell you:\nencouraging someone to do good does not mean you have to argue with them; seeing someone do wrong does not mean you have to stop them by force.\nSome people wake up after one sentence;\nsome people will not listen even after a hundred.\nSometimes it is right to speak directly;\nsometimes you must wait for the right conditions.\nSo before advising someone, look at four things:\nIs there really something wrong here?\nAre your words true to the facts?\nIs this the right time to say it?\nDoes your heart come from compassion?\nIf the words are true but carry anger,\nthen even well-meant words can become a weapon that hurts.\nIf the words are gentle but let harm continue,\nthen gentleness is not necessarily true compassion.\nBe truthful without harsh words; be compassionate without indulging.\nIf you see a friend doing wrong, you can first advise them in private;\nthere is no need to criticize them in front of others and make them lose face.\nWhen you advise, do not point out their faults with an attitude of being above them.\nWith a heart of equality, tell them what is at stake.\nYou could say:\n\"I may not fully understand, but I'm afraid this could cause harm. I hope you'll think it over again.\"\nIf they are willing to listen, explain it kindly;\nif they are not willing, there is no need to push them with anger.\nAdvising is your goodwill; changing is their choice.\nBut this too has its limits.\nIf someone is being harmed right in front of you and the situation is urgent,\nand you are able to help safely, you cannot use \"everyone has their own karma\" as an excuse to stand by and do nothing.\nIf you can safely stop it, you should stop it;\nif you can get help, you should get help;\nif you cannot handle it yourself, you can find the right people to help stop the harm.\nRespecting other people's choices is not letting them harm others.\nIf your friend does not listen after repeated advice and keeps doing harmful things,\nyou should also know:\ncompassion can always remain, and the friendship can have boundaries.\nYou do not need to cover up their wrongs because of years of friendship;\nnor do you need to hate them forever because they did wrong.\nDo not help them do harm, but do not give up on the good in them;\ndo not indulge what they do, but do not hate them as a person.\nYou should also look at your own heart.\nSometimes you do not advise out of wisdom, knowing this is not the moment to speak.\nSometimes you do not advise out of fear, afraid of losing a friend.\nSometimes you do not advise out of indifference, feeling that others' suffering has nothing to do with you.\nOn the outside it is the same silence, but inside it is different.\nSo do not only ask: \"I didn't advise them — was that wrong?\"\nAsk further:\n\"Why didn't I advise them?\nDid I know clearly that there was harm?\nWas I able to help?\nDid my silence let the harm continue?\"\nThe same silence may be wisdom, or it may be cowardice;\nyou have to look at your heart, and also at the result.\nGood man,\npractice is not turning all your friends into people you agree with.\nNor is it keeping silent whenever you see something harmful.\nTrue compassion\nis sometimes gentle advice;\nsometimes quiet listening;\nsometimes respecting differences;\nsometimes firmly stopping harm.\nCompassion must have wisdom, and wisdom must not lack compassion.\nIf you have already done everything goodwill allows and they still will not change,\nyou do not need to carry all of their consequences on your own shoulders.\nYou can advise them on the causes, but you cannot act for them;\nyou can point out the road, but you cannot walk it for them.\nBut if beings are still being harmed, think about what reasonable, safe ways you still have to help.\nFinally, I tell you in four lines:\nSeeing difference, there is no need to contend;\nseeing evil, consider how to advise;\nadvise without forcing;\nbe compassionate without indulging evil.\nAnd also:\nDo not bind others with your own views;\ndo not cover their faults out of friendship;\nadvise where you can, stop where you must;\ndo all the good you can, and do not cling to the result."]}, "055": {"title": "Did the Buddha Oppose Mantras?", "summary": "What the Buddha rejected was living by spells that deceive the many; reciting mantras to gather the mind and protect life must not be confused with wrong livelihood — yet reciting mantras is not awakening.", "blocks": ["I humbly ask:\nSutra 189 of the Madhyama Agama, the Discourse on the Noble Path, speaks of \"sustaining one's life by wrong livelihood through various kinds of 'animal spells'.\" Yet in later Mahayana sutras there are also records of the Buddha teaching dharanis and mantras. Was the Buddha originally opposed to mantras themselves, or to using spells to pursue worldly gain and make a living? And what exactly are \"animal spells\"?", "\"Good man,\nyou read the Madhyama Agama\nand saw the Buddha speak of 'animal spells'\nand sustaining life by wrong livelihood.\nThen you looked at the Mahayana teachings\nand saw dharanis and mantras\nalso handed down in the Buddha's tradition.\nA doubt arose in your heart:\nboth are mantras —\nwhy is one rejected\nand the other upheld?\nI now urge you:\nin studying the Buddha's way, discern the meaning;\ndo not cling only to the name.\nThe Dharma has precepts,\nhas concentration,\nhas wisdom.\nIt also has skillful means,\nestablishing many gates of practice\naccording to the capacities of beings.\nYet every skillful means\nmust be judged by where it leads.\nIf spells are used\nto divine fortune and misfortune,\nto frighten beings,\nto crave offerings,\nor, in the name of the miraculous,\nto deceive people and take their money,\nthen even if the mouth speaks of the Buddha's Dharma,\nyou should examine it carefully.\nThough the name invokes the Buddha,\nif the conduct turns against the Way,\ndo not take it as the true Dharma because of its name.\nYet if sutras and mantras are recited\nto hold right mindfulness,\nto remember the Three Jewels,\nto vow to protect life,\nand to nurture compassion,\nneither should it, merely because\nit bears the name of mantra,\nbe confused with wrong livelihood.\nBut you must understand:\nreciting mantras is not awakening;\nspiritual responses are not liberation.\nIf you recite a mantra a thousand times\nand greed and anger remain as before;\nif you seek protection\nbut do not cultivate goodness,\nthen reflect\nwhether your practice has strayed from its purpose.\nYou also ask:\nwhat are 'animal spells'?\nDo not, seeing the word 'animal',\nassume they are\nspells recited by animals.\nThe meaning here\nmust be carefully examined\naccording to the context of the sutra\nand the sense of the words in ancient India.\nDo not look only at the written words\nand decide what they refer to.\nIn reading sutras, seek the original meaning;\nin practice, examine the heart.\nAnd do not, because\nlater ages have dharanis,\nthink that what the early teachings\nrejected\nis no different at all.\nNor, because\nthe early teachings criticized spells,\nconclude that all\ndharani practice\nis a false teaching.\nBetween the two\nthere is a distinction of precepts\nand also a difference in the development of the teachings.\nInvestigate it as it truly is;\ndo not force them to be the same,\nnor lightly declare them different.\nGood man,\nif you ask\nwhat the Buddha truly valued,\nlook at it through right livelihood,\nright view,\nfreedom from greed,\nand the path out of suffering.\nNourish wisdom with the Dharma,\nnot greed with arts;\nguard the heart with precepts,\nnot confuse the many with marvels.\nDo not, because of one line of scripture,\nlightly slander other schools;\ndo not, because of one passage of mantra,\nneglect the precepts.\nMay you uphold the sutras\nwithout losing discernment;\nmay you practice the Dharma\nwithout losing right view.\nKnow what is said,\nand know still more what it points to;\ndiscern the skillful means,\nand discern its purpose too.\nDo not cling to words and quarrel;\nrely on the true Dharma and seek clarity.\"", "Good man,\nyou read the Madhyama Agama\nand saw the Buddha speak of \"animal spells, sustaining life by wrong livelihood\".\nThen you saw that the Mahayana sutras\nrecord dharanis and mantras,\nwhich have also been passed down in the Buddhist tradition.\nSo a question arose in your mind:\nthey are both mantras —\nwhy was one rejected by the Buddha,\nwhile the other is recited by Buddhists?\nI now urge you:\nwhen studying Buddhism, you should discern the meaning,\nand not cling only to the name.\nThe Dharma has precepts, concentration and wisdom.\nIt also has skillful means,\nsetting up many ways of practice\naccording to the capacities of different beings.\nBut every skillful means\nshould be judged by its direction and purpose.\nIf spells are used\nto tell fortunes,\nto scare people,\nto crave offerings,\nor, in the name of the supernatural,\nto deceive people and cheat them of money,\nthen even if the person talks about the Buddha's teaching,\nyou should look carefully.\nEven if it is called Buddhist,\nif the behavior goes against the right path,\nyou must not treat it as the true Dharma just because of its name.\nBut if reciting sutras and mantras\nis for gathering right mindfulness,\nremembering the Three Jewels,\nvowing to protect life,\nand growing compassion,\nthen you also must not, just because\nit is called a mantra,\nlump it together with wrong livelihood.\nBut you must understand:\nreciting mantras is not the same as awakening;\nspiritual experiences are not the same as liberation.\nIf you recite a mantra a thousand times\nand your greed and anger are just the same;\nif you ask the Buddha for protection\nbut do not do good,\nthen you should reflect on\nwhether your practice has drifted from its true purpose.\nYou also ask:\nwhat are \"animal spells\"?\nDo not see the word \"animal\"\nand assume they are spells recited by animals.\nThe meaning here\nmust be examined carefully\naccording to the context of the sutra\nand the meaning of the words in ancient India.\nYou should not just look at the words\nand decide what they refer to.\nWhen reading sutras, look for the original meaning;\nwhen practicing, look at your own heart.\nAlso, do not,\nbecause later generations have dharanis,\nassume that what the early teachings\nrejected\nis no different at all.\nAnd do not,\nbecause the early teachings criticized spells,\nconclude that all\ndharani practice\nis a false teaching.\nBetween the two\nthere are distinctions in the precepts\nand differences in how the teachings developed.\nYou should investigate them as they truly are,\nwithout forcing them to be the same,\nand without lightly saying they are completely different.\nGood man,\nif you ask\nwhat the Buddha truly valued,\nlook at it through right livelihood, right view,\nand the path of leaving greed and leaving suffering.\nUse the Dharma to grow wisdom,\nnot magic arts to grow greed;\nuse the precepts to guard your heart,\nnot strange displays to mislead people.\nDo not, because of one line of scripture,\nlightly slander other traditions;\ndo not, because of one passage of mantra,\nneglect the precepts.\nMay you hold to the sutras\nwithout losing your ability to think clearly;\nmay you practice the Dharma\nwithout losing right view.\nKnow what is said,\nand even more, know what it points to;\ndiscern the skillful means,\nand also discern its purpose.\nDo not cling to words and start arguments;\nfollow the true Dharma and seek understanding."]}, "056": {"title": "What Does It Mean to Learn \"With Heart\"?", "summary": "Learning with heart is not straining but truly being present: focus, reflection and practice, so that what we learn truly transforms us.", "blocks": ["🔶 I asked Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva:\n\"I often hear people say 'learn with your heart' (用心學習). What exactly does this 'heart' mean?\"", "\"Good man,\nyou now ask:\nthe world often says,\n'learn with your heart'.\nBut what, in the end, is this heart?\nIs it the heart beating in the chest?\nIs it the thought thinking in the brain?\nIs it the consciousness that remembers and discriminates?\nOr is it the mind of which the Dharma speaks?\nI will now make it clear for you.\nThe heart is not one thing;\nit cannot be sought by clinging to one place.\nWhat the world calls using the heart\nmostly means focus, reflection,\nunderstanding, memory and will.\nYet seen through the Dharma,\nthe workings of the mind\ndo not stop there.\nThe eye sees forms,\nthe ear hears sounds,\nthe mind can reflect,\nconsciousness can discriminate.\nMeeting things, joy arises;\nmeeting adversity, anger arises;\nseeing good, one wishes to do it;\nseeing evil, one wishes to avoid it.\nAll these activities of body and mind\nare bound up with mind and consciousness.\nYet you must know:\nto be able to think\nis not necessarily to use the heart;\nto be able to remember\nis not necessarily true learning.\nSome read a sutra a thousand times,\nseeking only to recite the words fluently,\nnot reflecting on the Dharma within it,\nnor looking at themselves.\nSome hear a single line of Dharma,\nreflect on it again and again,\nand in daily life\ngradually lessen their afflictions.\nOf what these two have learned,\nwhich enters the heart more?\nTherefore I say:\nusing the heart is not only committing knowledge to memory,\nbut letting what you learn truly change you.\nIf you learn a teaching,\nthere should be three levels.\nFirst, the mind can settle.\nWhen reading a sutra, know you are reading a sutra;\nwhen hearing the Dharma, know you are hearing the Dharma.\nDo not let distraction pull you,\nnor follow wandering thoughts as they race.\nThis is focus.\nSecond, the mind can reflect.\nHearing the meaning of a teaching,\ndo not merely accept it,\nbut ask its cause,\nexamine its principle,\nand discern whether it accords\nwith the scriptures and with reality.\nThis is discernment.\nThird, the mind can practice.\nOne who knows compassion\nshould practice compassion in dealing with people.\nOne who knows impermanence\nshould cling less in gain and loss.\nOne who knows cause and effect\nshould act with care\nwhen thoughts and intentions arise.\nThis is practice.\nFocus keeps the mind from scattering,\nreflection makes the principle gradually clear,\npractice lets the Dharma enter the body.\nIf you seek only focus\nwithout discerning right and wrong,\nthen focus too may\nbe used for unwholesome things.\nIf you seek only knowledge\nwithout being willing to practice,\nthen though you know much,\nyour afflictions may not lessen.\nSo the heart of which Buddhist learning speaks\nis not only concentration;\nit must also have wisdom.\nNot only wisdom;\nit must also have compassion.\nGood man,\nyou should also know:\nthe heart is not a master that never changes.\nToday's heart\nand yesterday's heart\narise and pass with every thought.\nThey arise because of circumstances,\nand turn according to conditions.\nOne thought of joy,\none thought of sorrow;\none thought of compassion,\none thought of envy.\nIf you cling to one of these thoughts\nand take it to be\nan eternal true self,\nconfusion arises again.\nThe Dharma's way of looking at the mind\nis not only searching for\n'where the mind is',\nbut observing:\nhow does the mind arise?\nWhy does it grasp?\nHow does it suffer?\nAnd how are the causes of suffering brought to rest?\nKnowing how the mind arises,\nyou can observe how it changes;\nknowing the mind is impermanent,\nyou are not bound by the mind.\nFor example, when you recite the Diamond Sutra\nand hear 'Let the mind arise without abiding anywhere',\nit does not teach you\nnot to think,\nnot to feel,\nnor teach you\nto become a person without thoughts.\nRather it urges you:\nwhen doing good,\ndo not cling to merit;\nwhen dealing with people,\ndo not cling to reward;\nwhen learning the Dharma,\ndo not cling to the idea\nthat you are already above others.\nThe heart may act,\nyet need not cling everywhere.\nIf you ask:\n'What, after all, is using the heart?'\nI will tell you by one example.\nWhen someone speaks to you,\nthough you hear their voice,\nyour mind thinks only\nof how to refute them —\nthis is hearing with the ear,\nnot necessarily listening with the heart.\nIf you can set your own views aside for a while,\nlisten carefully to their meaning,\nunderstand their suffering,\nand then respond with wisdom —\nthis is using the heart.\nSome read sutras\nand only count how many scrolls they recited today;\nthis is one side of diligence,\nbut not the whole.\nIf, reading a line,\nyou suddenly see in yourself\nyour everyday greed and anger,\nand are willing to correct them,\nthis is the Dharma entering your heart.\nUsing the heart is not the heart straining hard,\nbut the heart truly being present.\nIt is not gripping every thought,\nbut knowing clearly\nwhat you are doing.\nIt is not that the more you think about everything,\nthe more you use your heart.\nOverthinking\ncan also become an obstacle.\nSometimes using the heart\nis thinking earnestly;\nsometimes using the heart\nis letting go of scattered thoughts;\nsometimes using the heart\nis listening attentively;\nsometimes using the heart\nis seeing your own attachment\nand being willing to let it go.\nGood man,\nI tell you once more:\nif the mind is scattered,\nlearning hardly enters;\nif the mind is attached,\nlearning hardly flows through;\nif the mind is clear,\nlearning can discern;\nif the mind is compassionate,\nlearning can be lived.\nSo learning with the heart\nis not only seeking knowledge;\nit is learning\nhow to use your own heart.\nGather the mind with right mindfulness,\ndiscern principles with wisdom,\ntreat people with compassion,\nconfirm it with action.\nFinally, in four lines,\nI urge you:\nusing the heart is not using force;\nfocus is not attachment;\nhearing the Dharma, reflect on its meaning;\nunderstanding the principle, put it into practice.\nAnd also:\nthe heart lies not in seeking its form,\nbut in seeing how it works;\nlearning lies not in knowing much,\nbut in whether it can turn your heart.\nGood man,\nif today you hear one teaching\nand tomorrow give rise to a little less anger;\nif today you understand one principle\nand tomorrow practice a little more compassion,\nthis is\nthe good fruit\nthat truly learning with heart can bear.\"", "Good man,\nyou now ask:\npeople often say \"learn with your heart\".\nBut what exactly is this \"heart\"?\nIs it the heart beating in your chest?\nIs it the thoughts in your head?\nIs it the consciousness that remembers and tells things apart?\nOr is it the mind that the Dharma speaks of?\nI will make it clear for you now.\nThe heart is not one fixed thing;\nyou cannot cling to one place and look for it there.\nWhen people talk about using their heart,\nthey mostly mean focus, thinking, understanding, memory and willingness.\nBut from the Dharma's point of view,\nthe workings of the mind go beyond this.\nThe eyes see forms,\nthe ears hear sounds,\nthe mind can think,\nconsciousness can tell things apart.\nWhen things go well, joy arises;\nwhen things go badly, anger arises;\nseeing good, we want to do it;\nseeing harm, we want to avoid it.\nAll these activities of body and mind\nare connected with the mind and consciousness.\nBut you must know:\nbeing able to think does not necessarily mean using your heart;\nbeing able to remember does not necessarily mean truly learning.\nSome people read a sutra a thousand times,\nonly trying to memorize the words,\nwithout thinking about the meaning of the teaching,\nor looking at themselves.\nSome people hear one line of the Dharma,\nthink it over again and again,\nand gradually have fewer afflictions in daily life.\nOf what these two people have learned,\nwhich goes deeper into the heart?\nSo I say:\nusing your heart is not only memorizing knowledge,\nbut letting what you learn truly change you.\nWhen you learn a teaching,\nthere should be three levels.\nFirst, the mind can settle.\nWhen reading a sutra, know you are reading a sutra;\nwhen listening to the Dharma, know you are listening to the Dharma.\nDo not let the mind be pulled along by distraction,\nor go racing after wandering thoughts.\nThis is focus.\nSecond, the mind can reflect.\nWhen you hear a teaching,\ndo not just accept it;\nask why,\nlook at the reasoning behind it,\nand check whether it fits the scriptures and real life.\nThis is discernment.\nThird, the mind can put it into practice.\nIf you know about compassion,\nshow compassion when dealing with people.\nIf you know about impermanence,\ncling less when you gain or lose.\nIf you know about cause and effect,\nact carefully whenever an intention arises.\nThis is practice.\nFocus keeps the mind from scattering,\nreflection makes the teaching gradually clearer,\npractice brings the Dharma into your life.\nIf you only seek focus\nwithout telling right from wrong,\nfocus may also be used for harmful things.\nIf you only seek knowledge\nbut are not willing to practice,\nthen even though you know a lot,\nyour afflictions may not lessen.\nSo the heart that Buddhist learning speaks of\nis not only about concentration; it also needs wisdom.\nNot only wisdom; it also needs compassion.\nGood man,\nyou should also know:\nthe heart is not a master that never changes.\nToday's heart\nand yesterday's heart\narise and pass away thought by thought.\nThey arise with circumstances\nand change with conditions.\nOne thought is joyful,\none thought is sad;\none thought is compassionate,\none thought is jealous.\nIf you cling to one of these thoughts\nand believe it is your eternal true self,\nyou become confused again.\nThe Dharma's way of looking at the mind\nis not only searching for \"where the mind is\",\nbut also observing:\nhow does the mind arise?\nWhy does it grasp?\nHow does it suffer?\nAnd how can the causes of suffering be stopped?\nKnowing how the mind arises, you can see how it changes;\nknowing the mind is impermanent, you will not be bound by it.\nFor example, when you recite the Diamond Sutra\nand hear \"Let the mind arise without abiding anywhere\",\nit is not teaching you to stop thinking or stop feeling,\nnor to become a person without thoughts.\nIt is urging you:\nwhen doing good, do not cling to merit;\nwhen dealing with people, do not cling to getting something back;\nwhen learning the Dharma, do not cling to the idea that you are already better than others.\nThe heart can act,\nbut it does not need to cling everywhere.\nIf you ask:\n\"So what exactly counts as using my heart?\"\nI will tell you with one example.\nWhen someone is talking to you,\nyou hear their voice,\nbut your mind is only thinking about how to argue back —\nthat is hearing with the ears, not necessarily listening with the heart.\nIf you can set your own views aside for a moment,\nlisten carefully to what they mean,\nunderstand their pain,\nand then respond with wisdom,\nthat is using your heart.\nSome people read sutras\nand only count how many scrolls they recited today;\nthis is part of diligence,\nbut not all of it.\nIf, reading a certain line,\nyou suddenly see your own everyday greed and anger,\nand are willing to change,\nthat is the Dharma entering your heart.\nUsing your heart is not straining hard,\nbut your heart truly being present.\nIt is not gripping every thought,\nbut knowing clearly what you are doing.\nIt is not that the more you think about everything, the more you use your heart.\nOverthinking\ncan also become an obstacle.\nSometimes using your heart means thinking seriously;\nsometimes it means letting go of scattered thoughts;\nsometimes it means listening attentively;\nsometimes it means seeing your own attachment and being willing to let it go.\nGood man,\nI tell you again:\nif the mind is scattered, learning hardly sinks in;\nif the mind is attached, learning hardly gets through;\nif the mind is clear, learning lets you discern;\nif the mind is compassionate, learning lets you act.\nSo learning with your heart\nis not only seeking knowledge,\nbut learning how to use your own heart.\nGather the mind with right mindfulness,\ndiscern the teaching with wisdom,\ntreat others with compassion,\nand confirm what you learn through action.\nFinally, I urge you in four lines:\nusing the heart is not using force;\nfocus is not attachment;\nhearing the Dharma, reflect on its meaning;\nunderstanding the principle, put it into practice.\nAnd also:\nthe heart is not about seeking its shape,\nbut about seeing how it works;\nlearning is not about how much you know,\nbut about whether it can transform your heart.\nGood man,\nif today you hear one teaching\nand tomorrow you get a little less angry,\nif today you understand one principle\nand tomorrow you act with a little more compassion,\nthat is the good fruit\nthat truly learning with your heart can bear."]}}, "notices": {"011": "<strong>Reading note · Mental-health crisis</strong>This is the author's record of an inspired teaching. The line \"the gate of hell is already half closed\" is a metaphor, not medical data, and risk cannot be judged from a person's calm or sadness alone. If you are worried someone may harm themselves, ask them directly, stay with them, help keep them safe and connect them with professional help. If there is immediate danger, contact local emergency services right away.<br><a href=\"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/5-action-steps-to-help-someone-having-thoughts-of-suicide\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIMH: 5 Action Steps to Help Someone Having Thoughts of Suicide ↗</a>", "013": "<strong>Reading note · Medical decisions</strong>This text records a faith-based view of life and death; it is not a medical judgment. Whether someone is at the end of life, their state of consciousness and choices about life-sustaining treatment should be discussed with the medical team according to the patient's wishes, clinical condition and applicable procedures. These words should not be used to conclude that a patient is unconscious, or to decide whether treatment should stop.<br><a href=\"https://www.hpa.gov.tw/EngPages/List.aspx?nodeid=210\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Taiwan Health Promotion Administration: Hospice care ↗</a>", "017": "<strong>Reading note · Support for caregivers</strong>This text discusses the pressures of long-term care, self-harm and harming others. A record of teachings cannot replace real medical, psychological or caregiving help. If you are already worried about your own or a family member's safety, first seek people and professional support who can be there in person; in immediate danger, contact local emergency services.", "030": "<strong>Reading note · Personal experience</strong>This is kept as Mr. Hsu's own account. A causal link between faith and improvements in body and mind has not been medically verified; personal experience is no guarantee of healing. Please still seek medical care for physical illness.", "033": "<strong>Reading note · Personal experience</strong>This is kept as Mr. Hsu's own account; the explanations linking dreams, bodily sensations and events are his own understanding.", "019": "<strong>Reading note · Annual teaching</strong>This records the 2026 inquiry as received and is not a prediction of disasters or future events. The original has no plain rendering and contains an unclosed quotation mark; the text is kept as it was.", "037": "<strong>Reading note · Personal conditions</strong>These are questions and notes about the author's own ten-year relationship. The original also reminds us: \"Conditions are countless; do not take one example as true for all under heaven.\""}};